event: response.reasoning_summary_text.done
data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","summary_index":0,"item_id":"rs_02179466326755000000000000000000000ffffac15fd082e1d6a","output_index":0,"text":"Let me analyze this task. I need to design a CODESYS 3.5 ST FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task for a mechanical arm control with manual, single-step, single-cycle, continuous modes.\n\nLet me carefully parse requirements and assumptions.\n\nInterface: all BOOL inputs as listed, six BOOL outputs.\n\nKey semantics:\n- EmergencyStop: FALSE = stop triggered. So safe = EmergencyStop TRUE.\n- Mode selection mutually exclusive.\n- modeChanged: selected mode differs from previous invocation; FALSE for first invocation.\n- Emergency stop cancels sequence and outputs while retaining mode selection; recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose.\n- Start rising edge only; edges during active action ignored; edge history still updates.\n- No selected mode => all off and sequence reset.\n- Changing mode resets sequence and suppresses commands for that invocation.\n- Manual commands combinational, stop at limits; opposing commands inhibited; non-opposing actions may run together.\n- Automatic sequence phases: DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT.\n- Only one phase transition per invocation. In cycle modes, completion feedback switches to next command in same scan; next limit being already active suppresses its command but counted as completion next invocation.\n- Single-step completion switches all outputs off; next action requires fresh Start edge on later scan.\n- Continuous completion of LEFT wraps to DOWN in same scan. Single-cycle completion returns idle.\n- At start of new automatic sequence (action 1), UpLimit and LeftLimit must both be TRUE.\n- Grip/release complete on feedback switches.\n- Mode-change priority: emergency first; then mode change after first invocation clears sequence/latches and suppresses commands for that invocation; edge memories still update.\n- First invocation establishes mode and executes normally.\n\nNow details of phase transitions:\n- Phase DOWN: command MoveDown unless DownLimitSwitch. Completion when DownLimitSwitch.\n- Phase GRIP: ActivateGrip unless GripLimitSwitch. Completion when GripLimitSwitch.\n- Phase UP1: MoveUp unless UpLimitSwitch. Completion when UpLimitSwitch.\n- Phase RIGHT: MoveRight unless RightLimitSwitch. Completion when RightLimitSwitch.\n- Phase DOWN2: MoveDown unless DownLimitSwitch. Completion DownLimitSwitch.\n- Phase RELEASE: ActivateRelease unless ReleaseLimitSwitch. Completion ReleaseLimitSwitch.\n- Phase UP2: MoveUp unless UpLimitSwitch. Completion UpLimitSwitch.\n- Phase LEFT: MoveLeft unless LeftLimitSwitch. Completion LeftLimitSwitch.\n\n\"Completing phase switches to next command in same scan\" means in a scan where feedback is true, output should be the next phase's command (gated by its limit), and phase becomes next. E.g., pre-phase DOWN with DownLimitSwitch TRUE: outputs MoveUp? No — next after DOWN is GRIP, so ActivateGrip output in that scan. Output all other commands off.\n\nFor single step: when pre-busy and matching feedback true, then post busy false, all outputs off. Phase advances to next pre-phase for next time.\n\n\"step_done\" LTL: SingleStepMode AND pre-busy AND matching completion feedback -> off (P5). So in scan where completion occurs, all outputs off, busy false.\n\n\"waiting\": SingleStepMode AND NOT pre-busy AND NOT rising(Start) -> off. So nothing.\n\nSingle-step start: Start rising edge when idle (not busy) and top-left pose and selected auto mode. For single step, started -> post phase DOWN busy TRUE (P6). But at action 1 start, requires top-left pose both limits. For subsequent single steps, Start edge in waiting triggers next phase. Hmm \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE.\" So action 1 start requires top-left. Subsequent single step actions don't need pose check but need fresh Start edge.\n\nHow does phase progress in single-step waiting? We store next phase; on Start edge (idle, auto mode selected, and if phase = DOWN i.e. action 1 then top-left pose), busy becomes true, execute phase. Note phase should be stored even when idle after a step. First invocation next_step = 1 (DOWN).\n\nNeed to think about whether phase stored as \"next phase to execute\" while idle. The initial conditions given next_step=1, busy=false. There is also \"phase\" atomic proposition in cycle_done: \"pre-phase LEFT\". They mention next_step. We'll use enum/int NextAction 1..8.\n\nManual mode: commands combinational:\n- MoveDown = safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode & DownButton & !UpButton & !DownLimitSwitch\n- MoveUp = ... UpButton & !DownButton & !UpLimitSwitch\n- MoveLeft = LeftButton & !RightButton & !LeftLimitSwitch\n- MoveRight = RightButton & !LeftButton & !RightLimitSwitch\n- ActivateGrip = GripButton & !ReleaseButton & !GripLimitSwitch\n- ActivateRelease = ReleaseButton & !GripButton & !ReleaseLimitSwitch\n\nAlso emergency stop/no mode => off.\n\nMode change: suppress commands in the invocation where mode changed (but first invocation modeChanged FALSE). Reset sequence/latches. Edge memories update. So if mode changed (not first invocation): all outputs off, reset NextAction=1, busy false. Start edge memory still updated (so a Start held during mode change won't trigger after). If Start rising in that same scan... mode change suppresses commands, so no start. But edge history updated means next scan no fresh edge.\n\nAutomatic modes: SingleStep, SingleCycle, Continuous.\n\nSingle cycle: on Start edge when idle & top-left, start phase DOWN busy true. Then on completion each scan advances to next phase and outputs next command in same scan. At completion of LEFT (phase 8), single cycle returns to idle (busy false, NextAction=1? \"stops at the end of the cycle waiting for operator's instruction\"). Post idle all outputs off. P8: cycle_done -> idle & off in same scan. So phase 8 with LeftLimit true -> busy false, NextAction maybe 1 (DOWN) for next start; outputs off. But note P8 says at completion of left single cycle goes idle and off; while continuous goes begin_next (DOWN command gated by DownLimit). For continuous wrap, in same scan output MoveDown if !DownLimitSwitch. Hmm begin_next = \"post-phase DOWN with command gated by DownLimitSwitch\". So in the completion scan, phase becomes DOWN and MoveDown output asserted if not at down limit.\n\nIn continuous, when idle after emergency recovery? Continuous starts with Start edge from top-left. It runs forever.\n\nSingle step: Start edge triggers one phase, completion -> all off, busy false, NextAction advanced. Next Start works without top-left check except action 1's initial condition. Wait, how do we know a \"new automatic sequence\" vs subsequent step? When NextAction=1 (DOWN), require top-left pose. But after a full cycle in single-step, phase wraps from LEFT to DOWN; then next Start is new sequence requiring top-left pose. That matches.\n\nSo start condition: idle (busy false), selected automatic mode (SingleStep/SingleCycle/Continuous), rising Start, and if NextAction=1 then UpLimitSwitch & LeftLimitSwitch must both be true.\n\nThen busy true and execute current NextAction (which is 1 for DOWN).\n\nNow, \"Start edges during an active action are ignored; edge history still updates.\"\n\nMode change handling: selected mode is one of four, mutually exclusive. We store PrevMode (int 0..4 perhaps). On first invocation, modeChanged = FALSE. We need a FirstScan flag.\n\nMode change semantics: when selected mode differs from previous invocation, and not first invocation:\n- all outputs off this scan\n- sequence/latches cleared: NextAction=1, busy=false\n- edge memories updated (StartPrev := Start)\n- store PrevMode := selected mode.\n\nAlso what about mode change to no mode? \"No selected mode means all commands off and sequence reset.\" modeChanged definition: \"Selected mode differs from previous invocation\". If mode goes from manual to none, differs -> modeChanged true, off. Reset. Fine.\n\nWhat about EmergencyStop: \"EmergencyStop=FALSE cancels the sequence and outputs while retaining non-empty mode selection in arm_mode; changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence, and recovery requires a fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose (UpLimitSwitch & LeftLimitSwitch).\" Hmm this says emergency stop cancels sequence and outputs while retaining mode selection. Changing mode later clears outputs and resets sequence. Recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left.\n\nSo on emergency stop (safe false): outputs all off, sequence canceled => busy=false, NextAction=1? \"cancels the sequence and outputs\" yes reset sequence. Must require fresh Start edge from top-left. Need edge memory update while e-stop active to avoid stale edge. On release of e-stop, could a Start held high count as fresh edge? If Start was pressed during e-stop, edge history updates, so at recovery no rising edge -> safe. We should record StartPrev every scan including e-stop. Then recovery only when Start goes false->true after e-stop released, idle, top-left.\n\nDoes recovery require mode reselect? No, mode retained. Fresh start.\n\nPriority: emergency stop first; then mode change.\n\nNow \"No selected mode\" (none of four true) => off and sequence reset. modeChanged may be true when entering no mode; but also if no mode from first scan, modeChanged false but no_mode -> off and reset anyway. We can handle: if no mode, outputs off, reset busy, NextAction=1. Also mode memory still updates? selected mode 0. If no mode persists, modeChanged false. P1 uses no_mode -> off regardless.\n\nWhat about mode change between automatic modes while... suppressed anyway.\n\nManual mode with Start? Start irrelevant.\n\nNow think about outputs combination: \"Independent non-opposing actions may run together\" applies to manual only (combinational). Automatic only one command at a time. On a completion transition, output next command only, all others false.\n\nP4 reached limits: Every corresponding completion input suppresses its command. Important: even during modeChanged? off anyway. During manual, yes. During automatic, when limit reached we suppress command. But what about completion scan where next command is a different actuator whose limit is already reached? \"an already-active next limit suppresses its command but is processed as completion on the next invocation.\" So if next phase e.g. GRIP but GripLimitSwitch already true, ActivateGrip is suppressed; phase becomes GRIP; next invocation sees completion -> transitions to UP1. That means P4 holds globally: command outputs must be false whenever matching limit true. Our output logic should gate every command by its limit switch regardless.\n\nSo define:\nMoveDown := (some request for down) AND NOT DownLimitSwitch AND safe AND not modeChanged AND mode selected.\nMoveUp similarly, etc.\n\nRequests:\nManual mode:\n- down req = DownButton & !UpButton\n- up req = UpButton & !DownButton\n- left req = LeftButton & !RightButton\n- right req = RightButton & !LeftButton\n- grip req = GripButton & !ReleaseButton\n- release req = ReleaseButton & !GripButton\n\nAutomatic:\n- single step busy phase = DOWN: down req if DownLimit false\n- etc.\n- In completion scan for cycle modes, execute next phase's request.\n\nBut careful: at the completion scan for single-step, outputs are off, not next action. So no request.\n\nLet me formalize scan algorithm.\n\nState variables:\n- PrevMode : INT / enum (0 none,1 manual,2 step,3 single cycle,4 continuous)\n- FirstScan : BOOL := TRUE\n- NextAction : INT := 1 (1..8)\n- Busy : BOOL := FALSE\n- StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE (edge memory)\n- Maybe EStopPrev? Not required but could clear on e-stop.\n- Maybe ModeChanged output? Not interface, internal.\n\nOn each invocation:\n1. Determine selected mode int.\n2. Compute modeChanged := (NOT FirstScan) AND (SelectedMode <> PrevMode).\n3. Compute startRising := Start AND NOT StartPrev.\n\n4. Default outputs false, determine request flags.\n\n5. Priority:\nif NOT EmergencyStop (e-stop):\n   all outputs false\n   Busy := false\n   NextAction := 1\n   (maybe latch EStopLatched := TRUE?) The requirement: recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left. If we reset NextAction=1 and Busy=false, a Start edge after e-stop release with top-left works. But if Start is held across release, StartPrev updated each scan (StartPrev := Start), so no rising edge. Good. Do we need latched state? Possibly after e-stop, the arm might not be at top-left. Start requires top-left. Fine.\n   \nelif no mode:\n   all off, Busy:=false, NextAction:=1.\n\nelif modeChanged:\n   all off, Busy:=false, NextAction:=1.\n   \nelse:\n   case selected mode:\n   Manual:\n      Busy:=false; NextAction:=1? \"Manual mode: allows individual actions\". Should sequence reset when entering manual? mode change resets sequence. While manual persists, sequence irrelevant. Could set NextAction:=1 each scan; yes safe.\n      Outputs based on buttons & limits & opposing.\n   SingleStep:\n      if Busy:\n         // phase active, check completion feedback\n         if completion(currentAction) then\n             // all outputs off (default)\n             Busy := false\n             NextAction := next(currentAction)\n         else\n             request current action command (gated by matching limit - but feedback false by else, so limit false for matching)\n      else:\n         // idle/waiting\n         if startRising AND (NextAction <> 1 OR (UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch)) then\n             Busy := true\n             // execute NextAction in this scan: request command\n             request phase NextAction\n         else\n             all off\n      end\n      \n      Hmm: start condition for action 1 only top-left. NextAction indicates action to execute next. At initial NextAction=1. After completing action 1, NextAction=2 and idle; next Start executes action 2 without pose check. After completing action 8, NextAction=1 and idle; next Start requires top-left (new sequence). Good.\n      \n   SingleCycle / Continuous:\n      if Busy:\n         if completion(currentAction):\n             if currentAction = LEFT (8):\n                if Continuous:\n                   NextAction := 1 (DOWN); Busy remains true; request DOWN command in same scan\n                else (SingleCycle):\n                   Busy := false; NextAction := 1; all outputs off\n             else:\n                NextAction := next; request next command in same scan; Busy true\n         else\n             request current command\n      else:\n         if startRising AND top-left:\n             Busy := true; NextAction:=1; request DOWN\n         else all off, NextAction stays (should be 1 if idle after cycle)\n      end\n      \nBut wait: at cycle completion for non-left phases, we \"switch to the next command in that scan\". So in scan where completion true (pre-phase X), output next command. Phase becomes next. If next limit already true, command suppressed. Next scan sees completion of next phase and advances again. That's consistent.\n\nAt LEFT completion in continuous wraps to DOWN in same scan, output MoveDown if !DownLimitSwitch. Busy true, NextAction=1.\n\nSingle cycle at LEFT completion: idle & off. NextAction=1.\n\nContinuous start at initial top-left: DOWN. Note if DownLimit already true at start? Top-left implies DownLimit false presumably (contradictory limits outside contract). Output gated.\n\nNow P6 \"start -> started (post-phase DOWN, busy TRUE)\". For single-step after action 1, Start triggers action 2, not down! Hmm P6 formula: G(safe & !modeChanged & start -> started) where \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". So `start` proposition includes top-left pose. Thus only a start from top-left (new sequence) counts, and post-phase DOWN. In single-step, subsequent starts without top-left aren't \"start\" atomic proposition. Good. Our behavior aligns: only from top-left do we assert DOWN. But careful: what if single-step waiting with NextAction=2 and also top-left pose true (e.g., arm is up-left from UP1 phase)? Then Start edge with top-left and NextAction=2: the atomic `start` says fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected auto mode -> started (post-phase DOWN busy). Hmm conflict! Let's think. In single-step sequence: after UP1 (action 3), arm is at top, and if horizontal at left? Actually action 4 RIGHT hasn't happened yet, so at top-left after UP1? Wait sequence: DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT. After UP1, position top-left (if left). So if operator presses Start for action 4, with top-left pose and NextAction=4, `start` proposition is true per definition: fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode. And P6 would require started = post-phase DOWN busy TRUE. But expected action 4 is RIGHT. Hmm contradiction. Unless `start` means a fresh start of a new sequence, i.e., NextAction=1 too. The definition says \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". In single-step, each step requires a fresh Start edge; but at action 4, after UP1, arm at top-left. That would make P6 force DOWN incorrectly.\n\nMaybe assumption \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE\" clarifies start of new sequence. The atomic `start` may be interpreted as new sequence start (idle + top-left + action1). In single-step, when waiting for next action after action 3, idle and top-left; `Start` edge is not a \"start\" of sequence because NextAction != 1. Hmm the proposition wording lacks NextAction. But P6 says \"A permitted fresh start begins down.\" Maybe \"start\" should include sequence reset/idle boundary state. The initial conditions include next_step=1; likely formal model's `start` predicate is defined as: single step idle waiting and Start rising, then post-phase should be DOWN? Wait but that can't be for subsequent steps.\n\nLet me reread: \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". Perhaps for single-step subsequent actions, after each action the arm often is not top-left except after UP1/UP2. But action 3 UP1 completion leaves at top and left (since RIGHT not yet). Then action 4 Start edge would indeed be top-left. Unless... LEFT position means left limit; yes it started left and hasn't moved right. So true.\n\nHmm, but maybe in single-step mode after each step, the step sequence is reset? No, \"The mechanical arm automatically executes one action according to the preset program, and the operator needs to confirm after each action is completed to execute the next action.\" Preset program progresses.\n\nCould there be an interpretation where after each single-step action, Start acts as \"execute next\" not \"start sequence\", and the formal `start` proposition is only about idle after a completed cycle (NextAction=1)? The explanation \"A permitted fresh start begins down\" and \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step)\" strongly suggests start predicate needs NextAction=1. I'll implement accordingly; formal verifier will tell.\n\nAlso note P7 `ordered`: \"the next accepted automatic phase follows DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT\". Good.\n\nNow emergency stop interplay with outputs: on EStop false, off and reset. Also we should reset StartPrev memory each scan regardless.\n\nMode change detection: need to initialize PrevMode. First invocation: modeChanged false, PrevMode set to selected mode, then normal execution. If first invocation is manual buttons held, outputs should reflect buttons? \"The first invocation establishes the selected mode and executes normally.\" Yes.\n\nNo mode on first invocation: all off reset.\n\nLet me think about mode selection changes and Start edge. On modeChanged scan: clear sequence and suppress commands, edge memories update. So if Start rises exactly on mode-change scan, ignored, and StartPrev becomes true. On subsequent scans while Start still true, no edge. Operator must release and re-press. Good.\n\nOn e-stop scan: also update StartPrev. Sequence reset. Start edges ignored. After e-stop release with no mode change, if Start rising fresh from false->true and top-left -> start.\n\nNow what about Busy and NextAction reset on e-stop. NextAction:=1. Fine.\n\nNow need to define DUTs? Could implement with INT state and helper functions perhaps. Supporting objects allowed, but simplest single FB with VAR. Need to preserve exact interface. The declaration should be FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task and VAR sections. We can put internal variables in VAR. The instruction: \"declaration is CODESYS FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task followed by all VAR sections; omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK.\" So include input/output VAR sections and internal VAR.\n\nNeed supporting_objects list; can be empty. Probably easier empty unless we define enum. We can use INT constants. But maybe enum DUT nicer. To minimize compile risks, use INT values and local constants? CODESYS allows local CONSTANT VAR? e.g., VAR CONSTANT... END_VAR inside FB? Yes. But constants in declaration might be fine. Could simply use numeric comparisons. Let's define an enum DUT `E_ArmAction` maybe. Supporting object kind DUT with declaration. But compile may handle. Could also use INT. I'll use INT to be safe, with comments? Comments allowed in ST; user says no Markdown. Comments fine but not necessary. Better self-document.\n\nLet's design ST body carefully.\n\nVariables in declaration:\n\nVAR_INPUT\n    ManualMode : BOOL;\n    SingleStepMode : BOOL;\n    SingleCycleMode : BOOL;\n    ContinuousMode : BOOL;\n    EmergencyStop : BOOL;\n    Start : BOOL;\n    DownButton : BOOL;\n    UpButton : BOOL;\n    LeftButton : BOOL;\n    RightButton : BOOL;\n    GripButton : BOOL;\n    ReleaseButton : BOOL;\n    DownLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    UpLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    LeftLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    RightLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    GripLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    ReleaseLimitSwitch : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nVAR_OUTPUT\n    MoveDown : BOOL;\n    MoveUp : BOOL;\n    MoveLeft : BOOL;\n    MoveRight : BOOL;\n    ActivateGrip : BOOL;\n    ActivateRelease : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nVAR\n    PrevMode : INT := 0;\n    FirstScan : BOOL := TRUE;\n    NextAction : INT := 1;\n    Busy : BOOL := FALSE;\n    StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE;\n    SelectedMode : INT;\n    ModeChanged : BOOL;\n    StartEdge : BOOL;\n    Done : BOOL;\n    ReqDown, ReqUp, ReqLeft, ReqRight, ReqGrip, ReqRelease : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nDo we need more? We compute outputs default false each scan.\n\nImplementation:\n\nSelectedMode := 0;\nIF ManualMode THEN SelectedMode := 1;\nELSIF SingleStepMode THEN SelectedMode := 2;\nELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN SelectedMode := 3;\nELSIF ContinuousMode THEN SelectedMode := 4;\nEND_IF\n\nModeChanged := (NOT FirstScan) AND (SelectedMode <> PrevMode);\nStartEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev;\n\nReqDown := FALSE; ... all false.\n\nIF NOT EmergencyStop THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSIF SelectedMode = 0 THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSIF ModeChanged THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSE\n    CASE SelectedMode OF\n    1: // manual\n        Busy := FALSE;\n        NextAction := 1;\n        ReqDown := DownButton AND NOT UpButton;\n        ReqUp := UpButton AND NOT DownButton;\n        ReqLeft := LeftButton AND NOT RightButton;\n        ReqRight := RightButton AND NOT LeftButton;\n        ReqGrip := GripButton AND NOT ReleaseButton;\n        ReqRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT GripButton;\n    2: // single step\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := FALSE;\n            CASE NextAction OF\n                1: Done := DownLimitSwitch;\n                2: Done := GripLimitSwitch;\n                3: Done := UpLimitSwitch;\n                4: Done := RightLimitSwitch;\n                5: Done := DownLimitSwitch;\n                6: Done := ReleaseLimitSwitch;\n                7: Done := UpLimitSwitch;\n                8: Done := LeftLimitSwitch;\n            END_CASE\n            IF Done THEN\n                Busy := FALSE;\n                NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                IF NextAction > 8 THEN NextAction := 1; END_IF\n            ELSE\n                CASE NextAction OF\n                    1: ReqDown := TRUE;\n                    2: ReqGrip := TRUE;\n                    3: ReqUp := TRUE;\n                    4: ReqRight := TRUE;\n                    5: ReqDown := TRUE;\n                    6: ReqRelease := TRUE;\n                    7: ReqUp := TRUE;\n                    8: ReqLeft := TRUE;\n                END_CASE\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND ((NextAction <> 1) OR (UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch)) THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    3: // single cycle\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := ... CASE NextAction ...\n            IF Done THEN\n                IF NextAction = 8 THEN\n                    Busy := FALSE;\n                    NextAction := 1;\n                ELSE\n                    NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                    CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n                END_IF\n            ELSE\n                CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                NextAction := 1;\n                ReqDown := TRUE;\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    4: // continuous\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := ...\n            IF Done THEN\n                IF NextAction = 8 THEN\n                    NextAction := 1;\n                    Busy := TRUE;\n                    ReqDown := TRUE;\n                ELSE\n                    NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                    request...\n                END_IF\n            ELSE\n                request...\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                NextAction := 1;\n                ReqDown := TRUE;\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    END_CASE\nEND_IF\n\nThen output gating:\n\nMoveDown := ReqDown AND EmergencyStop AND (SelectedMode <> 0) AND NOT ModeChanged AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;\nMoveUp := ReqUp AND ... AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;\n...\nActivateGrip := ReqGrip AND ... AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;\nActivateRelease := ReqRelease AND ... AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;\n\nNote manual opposing: ReqDown excludes UpButton; and ReqUp excludes DownButton. Good. Grip/release likewise.\n\n\"all_auto_steps_valid\" etc.\n\nThen state update:\nPrevMode := SelectedMode;\nFirstScan := FALSE;\nStartPrev := Start;\n\nImportant: On mode change, selected mode differs from previous. On no mode, selected 0.\n\nNow question: mode change from none to manual: modeChanged true, outputs suppressed first manual scan. Assumption says \"then a change of mode after the first invocation clears sequence/latches and suppresses commands for that invocation\". Yes, entering any mode suppresses outputs for that invocation. Even from no mode to manual. Fine.\n\nEmergency stop \"retaining non-empty mode selection in arm_mode; changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\". If e-stop active, Inputs mode selection may change; do we set PrevMode to SelectedMode each scan? Yes. If mode changes during e-stop, then modeChanged was true but e-stop branch had priority; still PrevMode updates, so no later modeChanged. Is that okay? \"changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\" - during e-stop outputs already off and sequence reset, so no difference. Fine.\n\nNow think about P10: G((safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> other_manual) & ((safe & modeChanged) -> off)).\nother_manual defines Up/Left/Right/Grip/Release exactly as manual formula. Our logic matches except no mode? ManualMode true means selected mode 1. good. Note manual request in our Code does not include limit in request but output gating includes NOT limit, matching formula. opposing buttons exactly.\n\nP3 manual down formula doesn't include EStop? `safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> (down <-> down_manual)` where down_manual lacks safe. Matches.\n\nP1: G((!safe | no_mode) -> off). Our e-stop and no mode output gating off. Even if modeChanged false etc. yes.\n\nP2 exclusive: Our automatic only requests one command; manual interlocks; gating doesn't create opposite. Fine.\n\nP4 reached limits: output gating with NOT corresponding limit. Fine.\n\nP5 step wait: step_done = SingleStepMode & pre-busy & matching feedback. In that scan, our Busy pre-state true and Done true -> outputs none (req all false). Good. waiting = SingleStepMode & !pre-busy & !rising(Start) -> off. Our idle -> no req. Good. Note if Start rising and cannot start (not top-left), outputs off. `waiting` excludes rising so P5 doesn't demand. Fine.\n\nP6 automatic start: start -> started. Our start from top-left in auto starts down. But single step with NextAction != 1 and top-left pose presses Start -> we execute that action, not started! As discussed potential issue. Need to decide whether formal tool's `start` predicate includes NextAction=1. The tool's atomic proposition definitions likely compiled manually by benchmark authors: \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". If literally implemented without NextAction, our action 4 start from top-left could violate P6. But their reference implementation must satisfy it, and likely they included NextAction=1 or \"new sequence\". Let's examine wording in assumption: \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE.\" This implies NextAction=1 is known. P6 description \"A permitted fresh start begins down.\" A \"permitted fresh start\" is a new sequence. So likely `start` includes NextAction=1. I'll go with that.\n\nWait, but in single step after completing action 1 (DOWN), the arm is down; NextAction=2; Start executes GRIP. In idle, `start` prop per definition would require top-left pose, which is false, so P6 not triggered. After action 3 (UP1), top-left true and NextAction=4; press Start executes RIGHT. Physically at top-left but perhaps \"start\" should be false because NextAction != 1. If not, violation. Again likely includes sequence idle phase 1.\n\nCould avoid ambiguity by resetting NextAction? No, we must execute RIGHT after UP1 in single-step. So can't satisfy literal P6 otherwise. Trust assumption.\n\nNow think about \"Only one phase transition occurs per invocation.\" Our code one transition max. In continuous LEFT->DOWN same scan. Fine.\n\nNow about the state after single-step completion: NextAction advances. We set outputs off. Next Start executes next. Good.\n\nNow \"initial position top left\" doesn't force initial outputs; outputs zero initially. The FB var default outputs false. Good P_init.\n\nNow could formal verifier require no mode => sequence reset but not mode change? We do reset each no-mode scan. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: StartPrev update on very first scan. Start high on first scan: StartEdge false since StartPrev initialized FALSE; so no start. Is that expected? \"A Start press is a rising edge.\" First invocation with Start already TRUE — is that an edge? Probably no, because edge memory starts FALSE, so StartPrev FALSE -> StartEdge TRUE! Wait StartEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev, with StartPrev init FALSE. On first scan Start=TRUE gives StartEdge TRUE. Is that a rising edge? The memory starts FALSE, so yes technically. But \"fresh Start edge\" likely yes. The first invocation starts normally. \"The first invocation establishes the selected mode and executes normally.\" So if Start held on first scan and top-left, it may start. Hmm, is that acceptable? Test frameworks often reset instance and set Start false initially. First invocation with Start TRUE may be considered rising from initial false. Fine. In our initialization StartPrev FALSE -> edge. Good.\n\nNow think about modes exclusive assumption. No need to validate >1; caller keeps mutually exclusive. Our ELSIF priority would choose manual if multiple, but verifier won't test multiple per assumption (rejected). Fine.\n\nNow compile and simulation. Need use compile_st max 3 calls, simulate max 3, verify. Save final candidate.json. We have read-only FS but \"save exact final submitted source to candidate.json\" — likely we need write file; sandbox is read-only though tools perhaps? shell commands unnecessary. Hmm. We may need to use apply_patch? No write permitted? The environment says read-only, but the task says save candidate.json. Perhaps we can use shell? It says shell commands unnecessary but we might need to save. We can use apply_patch? apply_patch not listed as tool in this environment, only shell_command. Actually tools available: shell_command, plan, MCP compile/sim/verify, etc. No apply_patch tool despite developer mention. Sandbox read-only and approval never. We can't write via shell? Perhaps shell commands can write? Permission profile restricted read only, so no. But maybe the candidate.json is generated by harness? Hmm. We should attempt to write at end with shell_command? It may fail. Could use compile tool? No. Maybe there is an existing file? Let's inspect? The task says shell commands unnecessary. But requirement \"Save the exact final submitted source to candidate.json\" likely evaluated from our answer? We'll try write with PowerShell at the end; if denied, still return JSON. We can see.\n\nFirst let's maybe list files to see if candidate.json exists? \"Do not inspect other directories\" but current dir perhaps contains package. We shouldn't need. Let's start with compile_st. Need provide source object declaration, implementation, supporting_objects. Let's write clean ST.\n\nOne important CODESYS syntax nuance: Multiple variables on one VAR line `ReqDown, ReqUp, ... : BOOL;` is allowed in CODESYS? Yes, comma-separated identifiers allowed. But to be safe, one per line.\n\nCASE with integer. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `NOT EmergencyStop` etc.\n\nLet's consider output gating and modeChanged: If modeChanged true, req all false anyway; but gate too.\n\nWe might not need Done variable; but okay.\n\nLet's think about emergency stop definition: \"FALSE: Emergency stop triggered; TRUE: No emergency stop triggered.\" safe = EmergencyStop. Good.\n\nNow what about Start edge during active action: \"ignored; edge history still updates.\" We compute StartEdge every scan but only use in idle. StartPrev := Start at end. Good.\n\n\"Only one phase transition per invocation\" - In single step idle start, we set Busy true and request phase. That's a transition from idle to active. In continuous completion, one phase transition plus wrap. Fine.\n\nNow \"In cycle modes, completion feedback switches to the next command in that scan; an already-active next limit suppresses its command but is processed as completion on the next invocation.\" Our code: Done true -> NextAction++ and request next unconditionally (gated by limit in output). If next limit true, output gated off. Next scan Busy true, Done uses that limit -> transitions. Correct.\n\nBut what about at completion of phase 8 in continuous and DownLimit already true? Output MoveDown gated off; next scan Done down -> transition to GRIP. Correct.\n\nAt start (idle->DOWN) if DownLimit true (top-left shouldn't, but if contradictory) output gated off; next scan busy DOWN and Done -> GRIP. Fine.\n\nNow single cycle start requirement top-left: We require UpLimit & LeftLimit. Good. Note initial position top-left.\n\nSingle-step action 1 requirement top-left. Subsequent steps no check. Good.\n\nNow \"manual mode provided it is not yet in position\" — our down etc. checks matching limit. For grip, \"not yet in position\" GripLimitSwitch. Good.\n\n\"Independent non-opposing actions may run together\" — e.g., down + grip simultaneously; our manual requests independent. Opposing on same axis inhibited, grip/release inhibited. Vertical and horizontal allowed simultaneously. But wait limit-related P4 suppresses command if limit. Fine.\n\nNow potential interference: In manual, if DownButton and UpButton both pressed, both req false. P3 down_manual includes `DownButton AND NOT UpButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch`; our MoveDown = reqDown AND ... NOT DownLimit; reqDown = DownButton AND NOT UpButton, so both false. good.\n\nNow formal condition P10 other_manual for Up/Left/Right/Grip/Release. Our output gating also includes safe etc. Formula only when safe & not modeChanged. good.\n\nWhat about output gating `SelectedMode <> 0` redundant when ManualMode.\n\nNow let's think of scenario \"changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\" during e-stop. Our e-stop resets. Fine.\n\nOne subtlety: After e-stop, \"recovery requires a fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose\". But our Busy=false NextAction=1. After release, if Start edge high and top-left, starts. If mode is manual, Start irrelevant. If mode is no mode, off. Good.\n\nNow modeChanged should be FALSE for first invocation. We use FirstScan. On first scan if SelectedMode=0, modeChanged false; no mode off. Fine.\n\nDo we need to retain selected mode in arm_mode even if no mode? \"No selected mode means all commands off and sequence reset.\" fine.\n\nNow write implementation. Let's be meticulous about variable declarations and semicolons.\n\nOne nuance: In CODESYS, assigning outputs within CASE? We assign Req flags and compute outputs after. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: use of `ELSIF` supported. Yes.\n\nPotential issue: `CASE SelectedMode OF 1: ... 2: ... END_CASE` with statements. Good.\n\nLet's create helper procedure? Inline repetition. Could use helper methods but not necessary.\n\nNow formal verify might detect something. Let's think about P9 liveness. Formula: (G(safe & SingleCycleMode) & G(busy -> F feedback)) -> G(started -> F idle). Our single cycle runs through phases; if feedback eventually each busy, returns idle. Yes, assuming mode unchanged. If e-stop? Premise G(safe & SingleCycleMode) holds, so no. If mode changed? Then G(safe & SingleCycleMode) would be false after change, premise false. So fine. Need idle eventually after started. We progress. Good.\n\nBut subtle: P9 says feedback eventually for each busy; we do transition. okay.\n\nP8 cycle boundary: G((safe & !modeChanged) -> ((cycle_done -> (idle & off)) & (continuous_done -> begin_next))). cycle_done = SingleCycleMode AND pre-phase LEFT AND LeftLimitSwitch. Our if Done and NextAction=8 -> Busy=false, outputs off (req all false). idle post = post-sequence idle. yes. continuous_done -> begin_next = post-phase DOWN with command gated by DownLimit. Our NextAction=1 Busy true ReqDown true, gated. yes.\n\nP7 ordered. We follow.\n\nP6 start -> started. Need start predicate as interpreted. Fine.\n\nP5 step wait. In single step pre-busy and matching feedback -> off. Our Done branch outputs off. waiting -> off. Good.\n\nBut in single-step waiting, if Start rising but fails top-left for action 1, outputs off; fine since \"waiting\" excludes rising, P5 doesn't constrain. Maybe there are other specs? no.\n\nNow P3 manual down exact equivalence: G(safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> (down <-> down_manual)).\ndown = MoveDown output. down_manual = ManualMode AND DownButton AND NOT UpButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch.\nOur MoveDown when ManualMode & !modeChanged & safe = (DownButton AND NOT UpButton) AND NOT DownLimitSwitch. Exact. yes.\n\nP4 reached limits exact: our gating ensures any MoveDown true only if !DownLimitSwitch. yes.\n\nP2 exclusive: situations:\n- Manual down req and left req can be simultaneous (down+left) allowed; exclusive only opposes down/up and left/right and grip/release. Our per-axis opposing prevents both. good.\n- Automatic one req.\n- Could automatic req down and something else? Only one CASE branch. no.\n- In manual, could ReqDown and ReqUp both? ReqDown includes !UpButton, ReqUp includes !DownButton. no.\n- Grip/release same.\n\nNow one concern: outputs latching? We assign all outputs every scan after computing flags, so no stale. Good.\n\nNow declaration first line exactly `FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task`. Then VAR_INPUT etc. Omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. The JSON field declaration should include sections but no final END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. Fine.\n\nsupporting_objects [].\n\nLet's compile via mcp__plc__compile_st.","sequence_number":2920}

event: response.reasoning_summary_part.done
data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_part.done","item_id":"rs_02179466326755000000000000000000000ffffac15fd082e1d6a","output_index":0,"summary_index":0,"part":{"type":"summary_text","text":"Let me analyze this task. I need to design a CODESYS 3.5 ST FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task for a mechanical arm control with manual, single-step, single-cycle, continuous modes.\n\nLet me carefully parse requirements and assumptions.\n\nInterface: all BOOL inputs as listed, six BOOL outputs.\n\nKey semantics:\n- EmergencyStop: FALSE = stop triggered. So safe = EmergencyStop TRUE.\n- Mode selection mutually exclusive.\n- modeChanged: selected mode differs from previous invocation; FALSE for first invocation.\n- Emergency stop cancels sequence and outputs while retaining mode selection; recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose.\n- Start rising edge only; edges during active action ignored; edge history still updates.\n- No selected mode => all off and sequence reset.\n- Changing mode resets sequence and suppresses commands for that invocation.\n- Manual commands combinational, stop at limits; opposing commands inhibited; non-opposing actions may run together.\n- Automatic sequence phases: DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT.\n- Only one phase transition per invocation. In cycle modes, completion feedback switches to next command in same scan; next limit being already active suppresses its command but counted as completion next invocation.\n- Single-step completion switches all outputs off; next action requires fresh Start edge on later scan.\n- Continuous completion of LEFT wraps to DOWN in same scan. Single-cycle completion returns idle.\n- At start of new automatic sequence (action 1), UpLimit and LeftLimit must both be TRUE.\n- Grip/release complete on feedback switches.\n- Mode-change priority: emergency first; then mode change after first invocation clears sequence/latches and suppresses commands for that invocation; edge memories still update.\n- First invocation establishes mode and executes normally.\n\nNow details of phase transitions:\n- Phase DOWN: command MoveDown unless DownLimitSwitch. Completion when DownLimitSwitch.\n- Phase GRIP: ActivateGrip unless GripLimitSwitch. Completion when GripLimitSwitch.\n- Phase UP1: MoveUp unless UpLimitSwitch. Completion when UpLimitSwitch.\n- Phase RIGHT: MoveRight unless RightLimitSwitch. Completion when RightLimitSwitch.\n- Phase DOWN2: MoveDown unless DownLimitSwitch. Completion DownLimitSwitch.\n- Phase RELEASE: ActivateRelease unless ReleaseLimitSwitch. Completion ReleaseLimitSwitch.\n- Phase UP2: MoveUp unless UpLimitSwitch. Completion UpLimitSwitch.\n- Phase LEFT: MoveLeft unless LeftLimitSwitch. Completion LeftLimitSwitch.\n\n\"Completing phase switches to next command in same scan\" means in a scan where feedback is true, output should be the next phase's command (gated by its limit), and phase becomes next. E.g., pre-phase DOWN with DownLimitSwitch TRUE: outputs MoveUp? No — next after DOWN is GRIP, so ActivateGrip output in that scan. Output all other commands off.\n\nFor single step: when pre-busy and matching feedback true, then post busy false, all outputs off. Phase advances to next pre-phase for next time.\n\n\"step_done\" LTL: SingleStepMode AND pre-busy AND matching completion feedback -> off (P5). So in scan where completion occurs, all outputs off, busy false.\n\n\"waiting\": SingleStepMode AND NOT pre-busy AND NOT rising(Start) -> off. So nothing.\n\nSingle-step start: Start rising edge when idle (not busy) and top-left pose and selected auto mode. For single step, started -> post phase DOWN busy TRUE (P6). But at action 1 start, requires top-left pose both limits. For subsequent single steps, Start edge in waiting triggers next phase. Hmm \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE.\" So action 1 start requires top-left. Subsequent single step actions don't need pose check but need fresh Start edge.\n\nHow does phase progress in single-step waiting? We store next phase; on Start edge (idle, auto mode selected, and if phase = DOWN i.e. action 1 then top-left pose), busy becomes true, execute phase. Note phase should be stored even when idle after a step. First invocation next_step = 1 (DOWN).\n\nNeed to think about whether phase stored as \"next phase to execute\" while idle. The initial conditions given next_step=1, busy=false. There is also \"phase\" atomic proposition in cycle_done: \"pre-phase LEFT\". They mention next_step. We'll use enum/int NextAction 1..8.\n\nManual mode: commands combinational:\n- MoveDown = safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode & DownButton & !UpButton & !DownLimitSwitch\n- MoveUp = ... UpButton & !DownButton & !UpLimitSwitch\n- MoveLeft = LeftButton & !RightButton & !LeftLimitSwitch\n- MoveRight = RightButton & !LeftButton & !RightLimitSwitch\n- ActivateGrip = GripButton & !ReleaseButton & !GripLimitSwitch\n- ActivateRelease = ReleaseButton & !GripButton & !ReleaseLimitSwitch\n\nAlso emergency stop/no mode => off.\n\nMode change: suppress commands in the invocation where mode changed (but first invocation modeChanged FALSE). Reset sequence/latches. Edge memories update. So if mode changed (not first invocation): all outputs off, reset NextAction=1, busy false. Start edge memory still updated (so a Start held during mode change won't trigger after). If Start rising in that same scan... mode change suppresses commands, so no start. But edge history updated means next scan no fresh edge.\n\nAutomatic modes: SingleStep, SingleCycle, Continuous.\n\nSingle cycle: on Start edge when idle & top-left, start phase DOWN busy true. Then on completion each scan advances to next phase and outputs next command in same scan. At completion of LEFT (phase 8), single cycle returns to idle (busy false, NextAction=1? \"stops at the end of the cycle waiting for operator's instruction\"). Post idle all outputs off. P8: cycle_done -> idle & off in same scan. So phase 8 with LeftLimit true -> busy false, NextAction maybe 1 (DOWN) for next start; outputs off. But note P8 says at completion of left single cycle goes idle and off; while continuous goes begin_next (DOWN command gated by DownLimit). For continuous wrap, in same scan output MoveDown if !DownLimitSwitch. Hmm begin_next = \"post-phase DOWN with command gated by DownLimitSwitch\". So in the completion scan, phase becomes DOWN and MoveDown output asserted if not at down limit.\n\nIn continuous, when idle after emergency recovery? Continuous starts with Start edge from top-left. It runs forever.\n\nSingle step: Start edge triggers one phase, completion -> all off, busy false, NextAction advanced. Next Start works without top-left check except action 1's initial condition. Wait, how do we know a \"new automatic sequence\" vs subsequent step? When NextAction=1 (DOWN), require top-left pose. But after a full cycle in single-step, phase wraps from LEFT to DOWN; then next Start is new sequence requiring top-left pose. That matches.\n\nSo start condition: idle (busy false), selected automatic mode (SingleStep/SingleCycle/Continuous), rising Start, and if NextAction=1 then UpLimitSwitch & LeftLimitSwitch must both be true.\n\nThen busy true and execute current NextAction (which is 1 for DOWN).\n\nNow, \"Start edges during an active action are ignored; edge history still updates.\"\n\nMode change handling: selected mode is one of four, mutually exclusive. We store PrevMode (int 0..4 perhaps). On first invocation, modeChanged = FALSE. We need a FirstScan flag.\n\nMode change semantics: when selected mode differs from previous invocation, and not first invocation:\n- all outputs off this scan\n- sequence/latches cleared: NextAction=1, busy=false\n- edge memories updated (StartPrev := Start)\n- store PrevMode := selected mode.\n\nAlso what about mode change to no mode? \"No selected mode means all commands off and sequence reset.\" modeChanged definition: \"Selected mode differs from previous invocation\". If mode goes from manual to none, differs -> modeChanged true, off. Reset. Fine.\n\nWhat about EmergencyStop: \"EmergencyStop=FALSE cancels the sequence and outputs while retaining non-empty mode selection in arm_mode; changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence, and recovery requires a fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose (UpLimitSwitch & LeftLimitSwitch).\" Hmm this says emergency stop cancels sequence and outputs while retaining mode selection. Changing mode later clears outputs and resets sequence. Recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left.\n\nSo on emergency stop (safe false): outputs all off, sequence canceled => busy=false, NextAction=1? \"cancels the sequence and outputs\" yes reset sequence. Must require fresh Start edge from top-left. Need edge memory update while e-stop active to avoid stale edge. On release of e-stop, could a Start held high count as fresh edge? If Start was pressed during e-stop, edge history updates, so at recovery no rising edge -> safe. We should record StartPrev every scan including e-stop. Then recovery only when Start goes false->true after e-stop released, idle, top-left.\n\nDoes recovery require mode reselect? No, mode retained. Fresh start.\n\nPriority: emergency stop first; then mode change.\n\nNow \"No selected mode\" (none of four true) => off and sequence reset. modeChanged may be true when entering no mode; but also if no mode from first scan, modeChanged false but no_mode -> off and reset anyway. We can handle: if no mode, outputs off, reset busy, NextAction=1. Also mode memory still updates? selected mode 0. If no mode persists, modeChanged false. P1 uses no_mode -> off regardless.\n\nWhat about mode change between automatic modes while... suppressed anyway.\n\nManual mode with Start? Start irrelevant.\n\nNow think about outputs combination: \"Independent non-opposing actions may run together\" applies to manual only (combinational). Automatic only one command at a time. On a completion transition, output next command only, all others false.\n\nP4 reached limits: Every corresponding completion input suppresses its command. Important: even during modeChanged? off anyway. During manual, yes. During automatic, when limit reached we suppress command. But what about completion scan where next command is a different actuator whose limit is already reached? \"an already-active next limit suppresses its command but is processed as completion on the next invocation.\" So if next phase e.g. GRIP but GripLimitSwitch already true, ActivateGrip is suppressed; phase becomes GRIP; next invocation sees completion -> transitions to UP1. That means P4 holds globally: command outputs must be false whenever matching limit true. Our output logic should gate every command by its limit switch regardless.\n\nSo define:\nMoveDown := (some request for down) AND NOT DownLimitSwitch AND safe AND not modeChanged AND mode selected.\nMoveUp similarly, etc.\n\nRequests:\nManual mode:\n- down req = DownButton & !UpButton\n- up req = UpButton & !DownButton\n- left req = LeftButton & !RightButton\n- right req = RightButton & !LeftButton\n- grip req = GripButton & !ReleaseButton\n- release req = ReleaseButton & !GripButton\n\nAutomatic:\n- single step busy phase = DOWN: down req if DownLimit false\n- etc.\n- In completion scan for cycle modes, execute next phase's request.\n\nBut careful: at the completion scan for single-step, outputs are off, not next action. So no request.\n\nLet me formalize scan algorithm.\n\nState variables:\n- PrevMode : INT / enum (0 none,1 manual,2 step,3 single cycle,4 continuous)\n- FirstScan : BOOL := TRUE\n- NextAction : INT := 1 (1..8)\n- Busy : BOOL := FALSE\n- StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE (edge memory)\n- Maybe EStopPrev? Not required but could clear on e-stop.\n- Maybe ModeChanged output? Not interface, internal.\n\nOn each invocation:\n1. Determine selected mode int.\n2. Compute modeChanged := (NOT FirstScan) AND (SelectedMode <> PrevMode).\n3. Compute startRising := Start AND NOT StartPrev.\n\n4. Default outputs false, determine request flags.\n\n5. Priority:\nif NOT EmergencyStop (e-stop):\n   all outputs false\n   Busy := false\n   NextAction := 1\n   (maybe latch EStopLatched := TRUE?) The requirement: recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left. If we reset NextAction=1 and Busy=false, a Start edge after e-stop release with top-left works. But if Start is held across release, StartPrev updated each scan (StartPrev := Start), so no rising edge. Good. Do we need latched state? Possibly after e-stop, the arm might not be at top-left. Start requires top-left. Fine.\n   \nelif no mode:\n   all off, Busy:=false, NextAction:=1.\n\nelif modeChanged:\n   all off, Busy:=false, NextAction:=1.\n   \nelse:\n   case selected mode:\n   Manual:\n      Busy:=false; NextAction:=1? \"Manual mode: allows individual actions\". Should sequence reset when entering manual? mode change resets sequence. While manual persists, sequence irrelevant. Could set NextAction:=1 each scan; yes safe.\n      Outputs based on buttons & limits & opposing.\n   SingleStep:\n      if Busy:\n         // phase active, check completion feedback\n         if completion(currentAction) then\n             // all outputs off (default)\n             Busy := false\n             NextAction := next(currentAction)\n         else\n             request current action command (gated by matching limit - but feedback false by else, so limit false for matching)\n      else:\n         // idle/waiting\n         if startRising AND (NextAction <> 1 OR (UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch)) then\n             Busy := true\n             // execute NextAction in this scan: request command\n             request phase NextAction\n         else\n             all off\n      end\n      \n      Hmm: start condition for action 1 only top-left. NextAction indicates action to execute next. At initial NextAction=1. After completing action 1, NextAction=2 and idle; next Start executes action 2 without pose check. After completing action 8, NextAction=1 and idle; next Start requires top-left (new sequence). Good.\n      \n   SingleCycle / Continuous:\n      if Busy:\n         if completion(currentAction):\n             if currentAction = LEFT (8):\n                if Continuous:\n                   NextAction := 1 (DOWN); Busy remains true; request DOWN command in same scan\n                else (SingleCycle):\n                   Busy := false; NextAction := 1; all outputs off\n             else:\n                NextAction := next; request next command in same scan; Busy true\n         else\n             request current command\n      else:\n         if startRising AND top-left:\n             Busy := true; NextAction:=1; request DOWN\n         else all off, NextAction stays (should be 1 if idle after cycle)\n      end\n      \nBut wait: at cycle completion for non-left phases, we \"switch to the next command in that scan\". So in scan where completion true (pre-phase X), output next command. Phase becomes next. If next limit already true, command suppressed. Next scan sees completion of next phase and advances again. That's consistent.\n\nAt LEFT completion in continuous wraps to DOWN in same scan, output MoveDown if !DownLimitSwitch. Busy true, NextAction=1.\n\nSingle cycle at LEFT completion: idle & off. NextAction=1.\n\nContinuous start at initial top-left: DOWN. Note if DownLimit already true at start? Top-left implies DownLimit false presumably (contradictory limits outside contract). Output gated.\n\nNow P6 \"start -> started (post-phase DOWN, busy TRUE)\". For single-step after action 1, Start triggers action 2, not down! Hmm P6 formula: G(safe & !modeChanged & start -> started) where \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". So `start` proposition includes top-left pose. Thus only a start from top-left (new sequence) counts, and post-phase DOWN. In single-step, subsequent starts without top-left aren't \"start\" atomic proposition. Good. Our behavior aligns: only from top-left do we assert DOWN. But careful: what if single-step waiting with NextAction=2 and also top-left pose true (e.g., arm is up-left from UP1 phase)? Then Start edge with top-left and NextAction=2: the atomic `start` says fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected auto mode -> started (post-phase DOWN busy). Hmm conflict! Let's think. In single-step sequence: after UP1 (action 3), arm is at top, and if horizontal at left? Actually action 4 RIGHT hasn't happened yet, so at top-left after UP1? Wait sequence: DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT. After UP1, position top-left (if left). So if operator presses Start for action 4, with top-left pose and NextAction=4, `start` proposition is true per definition: fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode. And P6 would require started = post-phase DOWN busy TRUE. But expected action 4 is RIGHT. Hmm contradiction. Unless `start` means a fresh start of a new sequence, i.e., NextAction=1 too. The definition says \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". In single-step, each step requires a fresh Start edge; but at action 4, after UP1, arm at top-left. That would make P6 force DOWN incorrectly.\n\nMaybe assumption \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE\" clarifies start of new sequence. The atomic `start` may be interpreted as new sequence start (idle + top-left + action1). In single-step, when waiting for next action after action 3, idle and top-left; `Start` edge is not a \"start\" of sequence because NextAction != 1. Hmm the proposition wording lacks NextAction. But P6 says \"A permitted fresh start begins down.\" Maybe \"start\" should include sequence reset/idle boundary state. The initial conditions include next_step=1; likely formal model's `start` predicate is defined as: single step idle waiting and Start rising, then post-phase should be DOWN? Wait but that can't be for subsequent steps.\n\nLet me reread: \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". Perhaps for single-step subsequent actions, after each action the arm often is not top-left except after UP1/UP2. But action 3 UP1 completion leaves at top and left (since RIGHT not yet). Then action 4 Start edge would indeed be top-left. Unless... LEFT position means left limit; yes it started left and hasn't moved right. So true.\n\nHmm, but maybe in single-step mode after each step, the step sequence is reset? No, \"The mechanical arm automatically executes one action according to the preset program, and the operator needs to confirm after each action is completed to execute the next action.\" Preset program progresses.\n\nCould there be an interpretation where after each single-step action, Start acts as \"execute next\" not \"start sequence\", and the formal `start` proposition is only about idle after a completed cycle (NextAction=1)? The explanation \"A permitted fresh start begins down\" and \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step)\" strongly suggests start predicate needs NextAction=1. I'll implement accordingly; formal verifier will tell.\n\nAlso note P7 `ordered`: \"the next accepted automatic phase follows DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT\". Good.\n\nNow emergency stop interplay with outputs: on EStop false, off and reset. Also we should reset StartPrev memory each scan regardless.\n\nMode change detection: need to initialize PrevMode. First invocation: modeChanged false, PrevMode set to selected mode, then normal execution. If first invocation is manual buttons held, outputs should reflect buttons? \"The first invocation establishes the selected mode and executes normally.\" Yes.\n\nNo mode on first invocation: all off reset.\n\nLet me think about mode selection changes and Start edge. On modeChanged scan: clear sequence and suppress commands, edge memories update. So if Start rises exactly on mode-change scan, ignored, and StartPrev becomes true. On subsequent scans while Start still true, no edge. Operator must release and re-press. Good.\n\nOn e-stop scan: also update StartPrev. Sequence reset. Start edges ignored. After e-stop release with no mode change, if Start rising fresh from false->true and top-left -> start.\n\nNow what about Busy and NextAction reset on e-stop. NextAction:=1. Fine.\n\nNow need to define DUTs? Could implement with INT state and helper functions perhaps. Supporting objects allowed, but simplest single FB with VAR. Need to preserve exact interface. The declaration should be FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task and VAR sections. We can put internal variables in VAR. The instruction: \"declaration is CODESYS FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task followed by all VAR sections; omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK.\" So include input/output VAR sections and internal VAR.\n\nNeed supporting_objects list; can be empty. Probably easier empty unless we define enum. We can use INT constants. But maybe enum DUT nicer. To minimize compile risks, use INT values and local constants? CODESYS allows local CONSTANT VAR? e.g., VAR CONSTANT... END_VAR inside FB? Yes. But constants in declaration might be fine. Could simply use numeric comparisons. Let's define an enum DUT `E_ArmAction` maybe. Supporting object kind DUT with declaration. But compile may handle. Could also use INT. I'll use INT to be safe, with comments? Comments allowed in ST; user says no Markdown. Comments fine but not necessary. Better self-document.\n\nLet's design ST body carefully.\n\nVariables in declaration:\n\nVAR_INPUT\n    ManualMode : BOOL;\n    SingleStepMode : BOOL;\n    SingleCycleMode : BOOL;\n    ContinuousMode : BOOL;\n    EmergencyStop : BOOL;\n    Start : BOOL;\n    DownButton : BOOL;\n    UpButton : BOOL;\n    LeftButton : BOOL;\n    RightButton : BOOL;\n    GripButton : BOOL;\n    ReleaseButton : BOOL;\n    DownLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    UpLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    LeftLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    RightLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    GripLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    ReleaseLimitSwitch : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nVAR_OUTPUT\n    MoveDown : BOOL;\n    MoveUp : BOOL;\n    MoveLeft : BOOL;\n    MoveRight : BOOL;\n    ActivateGrip : BOOL;\n    ActivateRelease : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nVAR\n    PrevMode : INT := 0;\n    FirstScan : BOOL := TRUE;\n    NextAction : INT := 1;\n    Busy : BOOL := FALSE;\n    StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE;\n    SelectedMode : INT;\n    ModeChanged : BOOL;\n    StartEdge : BOOL;\n    Done : BOOL;\n    ReqDown, ReqUp, ReqLeft, ReqRight, ReqGrip, ReqRelease : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nDo we need more? We compute outputs default false each scan.\n\nImplementation:\n\nSelectedMode := 0;\nIF ManualMode THEN SelectedMode := 1;\nELSIF SingleStepMode THEN SelectedMode := 2;\nELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN SelectedMode := 3;\nELSIF ContinuousMode THEN SelectedMode := 4;\nEND_IF\n\nModeChanged := (NOT FirstScan) AND (SelectedMode <> PrevMode);\nStartEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev;\n\nReqDown := FALSE; ... all false.\n\nIF NOT EmergencyStop THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSIF SelectedMode = 0 THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSIF ModeChanged THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSE\n    CASE SelectedMode OF\n    1: // manual\n        Busy := FALSE;\n        NextAction := 1;\n        ReqDown := DownButton AND NOT UpButton;\n        ReqUp := UpButton AND NOT DownButton;\n        ReqLeft := LeftButton AND NOT RightButton;\n        ReqRight := RightButton AND NOT LeftButton;\n        ReqGrip := GripButton AND NOT ReleaseButton;\n        ReqRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT GripButton;\n    2: // single step\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := FALSE;\n            CASE NextAction OF\n                1: Done := DownLimitSwitch;\n                2: Done := GripLimitSwitch;\n                3: Done := UpLimitSwitch;\n                4: Done := RightLimitSwitch;\n                5: Done := DownLimitSwitch;\n                6: Done := ReleaseLimitSwitch;\n                7: Done := UpLimitSwitch;\n                8: Done := LeftLimitSwitch;\n            END_CASE\n            IF Done THEN\n                Busy := FALSE;\n                NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                IF NextAction > 8 THEN NextAction := 1; END_IF\n            ELSE\n                CASE NextAction OF\n                    1: ReqDown := TRUE;\n                    2: ReqGrip := TRUE;\n                    3: ReqUp := TRUE;\n                    4: ReqRight := TRUE;\n                    5: ReqDown := TRUE;\n                    6: ReqRelease := TRUE;\n                    7: ReqUp := TRUE;\n                    8: ReqLeft := TRUE;\n                END_CASE\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND ((NextAction <> 1) OR (UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch)) THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    3: // single cycle\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := ... CASE NextAction ...\n            IF Done THEN\n                IF NextAction = 8 THEN\n                    Busy := FALSE;\n                    NextAction := 1;\n                ELSE\n                    NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                    CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n                END_IF\n            ELSE\n                CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                NextAction := 1;\n                ReqDown := TRUE;\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    4: // continuous\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := ...\n            IF Done THEN\n                IF NextAction = 8 THEN\n                    NextAction := 1;\n                    Busy := TRUE;\n                    ReqDown := TRUE;\n                ELSE\n                    NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                    request...\n                END_IF\n            ELSE\n                request...\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                NextAction := 1;\n                ReqDown := TRUE;\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    END_CASE\nEND_IF\n\nThen output gating:\n\nMoveDown := ReqDown AND EmergencyStop AND (SelectedMode <> 0) AND NOT ModeChanged AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;\nMoveUp := ReqUp AND ... AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;\n...\nActivateGrip := ReqGrip AND ... AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;\nActivateRelease := ReqRelease AND ... AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;\n\nNote manual opposing: ReqDown excludes UpButton; and ReqUp excludes DownButton. Good. Grip/release likewise.\n\n\"all_auto_steps_valid\" etc.\n\nThen state update:\nPrevMode := SelectedMode;\nFirstScan := FALSE;\nStartPrev := Start;\n\nImportant: On mode change, selected mode differs from previous. On no mode, selected 0.\n\nNow question: mode change from none to manual: modeChanged true, outputs suppressed first manual scan. Assumption says \"then a change of mode after the first invocation clears sequence/latches and suppresses commands for that invocation\". Yes, entering any mode suppresses outputs for that invocation. Even from no mode to manual. Fine.\n\nEmergency stop \"retaining non-empty mode selection in arm_mode; changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\". If e-stop active, Inputs mode selection may change; do we set PrevMode to SelectedMode each scan? Yes. If mode changes during e-stop, then modeChanged was true but e-stop branch had priority; still PrevMode updates, so no later modeChanged. Is that okay? \"changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\" - during e-stop outputs already off and sequence reset, so no difference. Fine.\n\nNow think about P10: G((safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> other_manual) & ((safe & modeChanged) -> off)).\nother_manual defines Up/Left/Right/Grip/Release exactly as manual formula. Our logic matches except no mode? ManualMode true means selected mode 1. good. Note manual request in our Code does not include limit in request but output gating includes NOT limit, matching formula. opposing buttons exactly.\n\nP3 manual down formula doesn't include EStop? `safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> (down <-> down_manual)` where down_manual lacks safe. Matches.\n\nP1: G((!safe | no_mode) -> off). Our e-stop and no mode output gating off. Even if modeChanged false etc. yes.\n\nP2 exclusive: Our automatic only requests one command; manual interlocks; gating doesn't create opposite. Fine.\n\nP4 reached limits: output gating with NOT corresponding limit. Fine.\n\nP5 step wait: step_done = SingleStepMode & pre-busy & matching feedback. In that scan, our Busy pre-state true and Done true -> outputs none (req all false). Good. waiting = SingleStepMode & !pre-busy & !rising(Start) -> off. Our idle -> no req. Good. Note if Start rising and cannot start (not top-left), outputs off. `waiting` excludes rising so P5 doesn't demand. Fine.\n\nP6 automatic start: start -> started. Our start from top-left in auto starts down. But single step with NextAction != 1 and top-left pose presses Start -> we execute that action, not started! As discussed potential issue. Need to decide whether formal tool's `start` predicate includes NextAction=1. The tool's atomic proposition definitions likely compiled manually by benchmark authors: \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". If literally implemented without NextAction, our action 4 start from top-left could violate P6. But their reference implementation must satisfy it, and likely they included NextAction=1 or \"new sequence\". Let's examine wording in assumption: \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE.\" This implies NextAction=1 is known. P6 description \"A permitted fresh start begins down.\" A \"permitted fresh start\" is a new sequence. So likely `start` includes NextAction=1. I'll go with that.\n\nWait, but in single step after completing action 1 (DOWN), the arm is down; NextAction=2; Start executes GRIP. In idle, `start` prop per definition would require top-left pose, which is false, so P6 not triggered. After action 3 (UP1), top-left true and NextAction=4; press Start executes RIGHT. Physically at top-left but perhaps \"start\" should be false because NextAction != 1. If not, violation. Again likely includes sequence idle phase 1.\n\nCould avoid ambiguity by resetting NextAction? No, we must execute RIGHT after UP1 in single-step. So can't satisfy literal P6 otherwise. Trust assumption.\n\nNow think about \"Only one phase transition occurs per invocation.\" Our code one transition max. In continuous LEFT->DOWN same scan. Fine.\n\nNow about the state after single-step completion: NextAction advances. We set outputs off. Next Start executes next. Good.\n\nNow \"initial position top left\" doesn't force initial outputs; outputs zero initially. The FB var default outputs false. Good P_init.\n\nNow could formal verifier require no mode => sequence reset but not mode change? We do reset each no-mode scan. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: StartPrev update on very first scan. Start high on first scan: StartEdge false since StartPrev initialized FALSE; so no start. Is that expected? \"A Start press is a rising edge.\" First invocation with Start already TRUE — is that an edge? Probably no, because edge memory starts FALSE, so StartPrev FALSE -> StartEdge TRUE! Wait StartEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev, with StartPrev init FALSE. On first scan Start=TRUE gives StartEdge TRUE. Is that a rising edge? The memory starts FALSE, so yes technically. But \"fresh Start edge\" likely yes. The first invocation starts normally. \"The first invocation establishes the selected mode and executes normally.\" So if Start held on first scan and top-left, it may start. Hmm, is that acceptable? Test frameworks often reset instance and set Start false initially. First invocation with Start TRUE may be considered rising from initial false. Fine. In our initialization StartPrev FALSE -> edge. Good.\n\nNow think about modes exclusive assumption. No need to validate >1; caller keeps mutually exclusive. Our ELSIF priority would choose manual if multiple, but verifier won't test multiple per assumption (rejected). Fine.\n\nNow compile and simulation. Need use compile_st max 3 calls, simulate max 3, verify. Save final candidate.json. We have read-only FS but \"save exact final submitted source to candidate.json\" — likely we need write file; sandbox is read-only though tools perhaps? shell commands unnecessary. Hmm. We may need to use apply_patch? No write permitted? The environment says read-only, but the task says save candidate.json. Perhaps we can use shell? It says shell commands unnecessary but we might need to save. We can use apply_patch? apply_patch not listed as tool in this environment, only shell_command. Actually tools available: shell_command, plan, MCP compile/sim/verify, etc. No apply_patch tool despite developer mention. Sandbox read-only and approval never. We can't write via shell? Perhaps shell commands can write? Permission profile restricted read only, so no. But maybe the candidate.json is generated by harness? Hmm. We should attempt to write at end with shell_command? It may fail. Could use compile tool? No. Maybe there is an existing file? Let's inspect? The task says shell commands unnecessary. But requirement \"Save the exact final submitted source to candidate.json\" likely evaluated from our answer? We'll try write with PowerShell at the end; if denied, still return JSON. We can see.\n\nFirst let's maybe list files to see if candidate.json exists? \"Do not inspect other directories\" but current dir perhaps contains package. We shouldn't need. Let's start with compile_st. Need provide source object declaration, implementation, supporting_objects. Let's write clean ST.\n\nOne important CODESYS syntax nuance: Multiple variables on one VAR line `ReqDown, ReqUp, ... : BOOL;` is allowed in CODESYS? Yes, comma-separated identifiers allowed. But to be safe, one per line.\n\nCASE with integer. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `NOT EmergencyStop` etc.\n\nLet's consider output gating and modeChanged: If modeChanged true, req all false anyway; but gate too.\n\nWe might not need Done variable; but okay.\n\nLet's think about emergency stop definition: \"FALSE: Emergency stop triggered; TRUE: No emergency stop triggered.\" safe = EmergencyStop. Good.\n\nNow what about Start edge during active action: \"ignored; edge history still updates.\" We compute StartEdge every scan but only use in idle. StartPrev := Start at end. Good.\n\n\"Only one phase transition per invocation\" - In single step idle start, we set Busy true and request phase. That's a transition from idle to active. In continuous completion, one phase transition plus wrap. Fine.\n\nNow \"In cycle modes, completion feedback switches to the next command in that scan; an already-active next limit suppresses its command but is processed as completion on the next invocation.\" Our code: Done true -> NextAction++ and request next unconditionally (gated by limit in output). If next limit true, output gated off. Next scan Busy true, Done uses that limit -> transitions. Correct.\n\nBut what about at completion of phase 8 in continuous and DownLimit already true? Output MoveDown gated off; next scan Done down -> transition to GRIP. Correct.\n\nAt start (idle->DOWN) if DownLimit true (top-left shouldn't, but if contradictory) output gated off; next scan busy DOWN and Done -> GRIP. Fine.\n\nNow single cycle start requirement top-left: We require UpLimit & LeftLimit. Good. Note initial position top-left.\n\nSingle-step action 1 requirement top-left. Subsequent steps no check. Good.\n\nNow \"manual mode provided it is not yet in position\" — our down etc. checks matching limit. For grip, \"not yet in position\" GripLimitSwitch. Good.\n\n\"Independent non-opposing actions may run together\" — e.g., down + grip simultaneously; our manual requests independent. Opposing on same axis inhibited, grip/release inhibited. Vertical and horizontal allowed simultaneously. But wait limit-related P4 suppresses command if limit. Fine.\n\nNow potential interference: In manual, if DownButton and UpButton both pressed, both req false. P3 down_manual includes `DownButton AND NOT UpButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch`; our MoveDown = reqDown AND ... NOT DownLimit; reqDown = DownButton AND NOT UpButton, so both false. good.\n\nNow formal condition P10 other_manual for Up/Left/Right/Grip/Release. Our output gating also includes safe etc. Formula only when safe & not modeChanged. good.\n\nWhat about output gating `SelectedMode <> 0` redundant when ManualMode.\n\nNow let's think of scenario \"changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\" during e-stop. Our e-stop resets. Fine.\n\nOne subtlety: After e-stop, \"recovery requires a fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose\". But our Busy=false NextAction=1. After release, if Start edge high and top-left, starts. If mode is manual, Start irrelevant. If mode is no mode, off. Good.\n\nNow modeChanged should be FALSE for first invocation. We use FirstScan. On first scan if SelectedMode=0, modeChanged false; no mode off. Fine.\n\nDo we need to retain selected mode in arm_mode even if no mode? \"No selected mode means all commands off and sequence reset.\" fine.\n\nNow write implementation. Let's be meticulous about variable declarations and semicolons.\n\nOne nuance: In CODESYS, assigning outputs within CASE? We assign Req flags and compute outputs after. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: use of `ELSIF` supported. Yes.\n\nPotential issue: `CASE SelectedMode OF 1: ... 2: ... END_CASE` with statements. Good.\n\nLet's create helper procedure? Inline repetition. Could use helper methods but not necessary.\n\nNow formal verify might detect something. Let's think about P9 liveness. Formula: (G(safe & SingleCycleMode) & G(busy -> F feedback)) -> G(started -> F idle). Our single cycle runs through phases; if feedback eventually each busy, returns idle. Yes, assuming mode unchanged. If e-stop? Premise G(safe & SingleCycleMode) holds, so no. If mode changed? Then G(safe & SingleCycleMode) would be false after change, premise false. So fine. Need idle eventually after started. We progress. Good.\n\nBut subtle: P9 says feedback eventually for each busy; we do transition. okay.\n\nP8 cycle boundary: G((safe & !modeChanged) -> ((cycle_done -> (idle & off)) & (continuous_done -> begin_next))). cycle_done = SingleCycleMode AND pre-phase LEFT AND LeftLimitSwitch. Our if Done and NextAction=8 -> Busy=false, outputs off (req all false). idle post = post-sequence idle. yes. continuous_done -> begin_next = post-phase DOWN with command gated by DownLimit. Our NextAction=1 Busy true ReqDown true, gated. yes.\n\nP7 ordered. We follow.\n\nP6 start -> started. Need start predicate as interpreted. Fine.\n\nP5 step wait. In single step pre-busy and matching feedback -> off. Our Done branch outputs off. waiting -> off. Good.\n\nBut in single-step waiting, if Start rising but fails top-left for action 1, outputs off; fine since \"waiting\" excludes rising, P5 doesn't constrain. Maybe there are other specs? no.\n\nNow P3 manual down exact equivalence: G(safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> (down <-> down_manual)).\ndown = MoveDown output. down_manual = ManualMode AND DownButton AND NOT UpButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch.\nOur MoveDown when ManualMode & !modeChanged & safe = (DownButton AND NOT UpButton) AND NOT DownLimitSwitch. Exact. yes.\n\nP4 reached limits exact: our gating ensures any MoveDown true only if !DownLimitSwitch. yes.\n\nP2 exclusive: situations:\n- Manual down req and left req can be simultaneous (down+left) allowed; exclusive only opposes down/up and left/right and grip/release. Our per-axis opposing prevents both. good.\n- Automatic one req.\n- Could automatic req down and something else? Only one CASE branch. no.\n- In manual, could ReqDown and ReqUp both? ReqDown includes !UpButton, ReqUp includes !DownButton. no.\n- Grip/release same.\n\nNow one concern: outputs latching? We assign all outputs every scan after computing flags, so no stale. Good.\n\nNow declaration first line exactly `FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task`. Then VAR_INPUT etc. Omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. The JSON field declaration should include sections but no final END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. Fine.\n\nsupporting_objects [].\n\nLet's compile via mcp__plc__compile_st."},"sequence_number":2921}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","output_index":0,"item":{"id":"rs_02179466326755000000000000000000000ffffac15fd082e1d6a","type":"reasoning","summary":[{"type":"summary_text","text":"Let me analyze this task. I need to design a CODESYS 3.5 ST FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task for a mechanical arm control with manual, single-step, single-cycle, continuous modes.\n\nLet me carefully parse requirements and assumptions.\n\nInterface: all BOOL inputs as listed, six BOOL outputs.\n\nKey semantics:\n- EmergencyStop: FALSE = stop triggered. So safe = EmergencyStop TRUE.\n- Mode selection mutually exclusive.\n- modeChanged: selected mode differs from previous invocation; FALSE for first invocation.\n- Emergency stop cancels sequence and outputs while retaining mode selection; recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose.\n- Start rising edge only; edges during active action ignored; edge history still updates.\n- No selected mode => all off and sequence reset.\n- Changing mode resets sequence and suppresses commands for that invocation.\n- Manual commands combinational, stop at limits; opposing commands inhibited; non-opposing actions may run together.\n- Automatic sequence phases: DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT.\n- Only one phase transition per invocation. In cycle modes, completion feedback switches to next command in same scan; next limit being already active suppresses its command but counted as completion next invocation.\n- Single-step completion switches all outputs off; next action requires fresh Start edge on later scan.\n- Continuous completion of LEFT wraps to DOWN in same scan. Single-cycle completion returns idle.\n- At start of new automatic sequence (action 1), UpLimit and LeftLimit must both be TRUE.\n- Grip/release complete on feedback switches.\n- Mode-change priority: emergency first; then mode change after first invocation clears sequence/latches and suppresses commands for that invocation; edge memories still update.\n- First invocation establishes mode and executes normally.\n\nNow details of phase transitions:\n- Phase DOWN: command MoveDown unless DownLimitSwitch. Completion when DownLimitSwitch.\n- Phase GRIP: ActivateGrip unless GripLimitSwitch. Completion when GripLimitSwitch.\n- Phase UP1: MoveUp unless UpLimitSwitch. Completion when UpLimitSwitch.\n- Phase RIGHT: MoveRight unless RightLimitSwitch. Completion when RightLimitSwitch.\n- Phase DOWN2: MoveDown unless DownLimitSwitch. Completion DownLimitSwitch.\n- Phase RELEASE: ActivateRelease unless ReleaseLimitSwitch. Completion ReleaseLimitSwitch.\n- Phase UP2: MoveUp unless UpLimitSwitch. Completion UpLimitSwitch.\n- Phase LEFT: MoveLeft unless LeftLimitSwitch. Completion LeftLimitSwitch.\n\n\"Completing phase switches to next command in same scan\" means in a scan where feedback is true, output should be the next phase's command (gated by its limit), and phase becomes next. E.g., pre-phase DOWN with DownLimitSwitch TRUE: outputs MoveUp? No — next after DOWN is GRIP, so ActivateGrip output in that scan. Output all other commands off.\n\nFor single step: when pre-busy and matching feedback true, then post busy false, all outputs off. Phase advances to next pre-phase for next time.\n\n\"step_done\" LTL: SingleStepMode AND pre-busy AND matching completion feedback -> off (P5). So in scan where completion occurs, all outputs off, busy false.\n\n\"waiting\": SingleStepMode AND NOT pre-busy AND NOT rising(Start) -> off. So nothing.\n\nSingle-step start: Start rising edge when idle (not busy) and top-left pose and selected auto mode. For single step, started -> post phase DOWN busy TRUE (P6). But at action 1 start, requires top-left pose both limits. For subsequent single steps, Start edge in waiting triggers next phase. Hmm \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE.\" So action 1 start requires top-left. Subsequent single step actions don't need pose check but need fresh Start edge.\n\nHow does phase progress in single-step waiting? We store next phase; on Start edge (idle, auto mode selected, and if phase = DOWN i.e. action 1 then top-left pose), busy becomes true, execute phase. Note phase should be stored even when idle after a step. First invocation next_step = 1 (DOWN).\n\nNeed to think about whether phase stored as \"next phase to execute\" while idle. The initial conditions given next_step=1, busy=false. There is also \"phase\" atomic proposition in cycle_done: \"pre-phase LEFT\". They mention next_step. We'll use enum/int NextAction 1..8.\n\nManual mode: commands combinational:\n- MoveDown = safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode & DownButton & !UpButton & !DownLimitSwitch\n- MoveUp = ... UpButton & !DownButton & !UpLimitSwitch\n- MoveLeft = LeftButton & !RightButton & !LeftLimitSwitch\n- MoveRight = RightButton & !LeftButton & !RightLimitSwitch\n- ActivateGrip = GripButton & !ReleaseButton & !GripLimitSwitch\n- ActivateRelease = ReleaseButton & !GripButton & !ReleaseLimitSwitch\n\nAlso emergency stop/no mode => off.\n\nMode change: suppress commands in the invocation where mode changed (but first invocation modeChanged FALSE). Reset sequence/latches. Edge memories update. So if mode changed (not first invocation): all outputs off, reset NextAction=1, busy false. Start edge memory still updated (so a Start held during mode change won't trigger after). If Start rising in that same scan... mode change suppresses commands, so no start. But edge history updated means next scan no fresh edge.\n\nAutomatic modes: SingleStep, SingleCycle, Continuous.\n\nSingle cycle: on Start edge when idle & top-left, start phase DOWN busy true. Then on completion each scan advances to next phase and outputs next command in same scan. At completion of LEFT (phase 8), single cycle returns to idle (busy false, NextAction=1? \"stops at the end of the cycle waiting for operator's instruction\"). Post idle all outputs off. P8: cycle_done -> idle & off in same scan. So phase 8 with LeftLimit true -> busy false, NextAction maybe 1 (DOWN) for next start; outputs off. But note P8 says at completion of left single cycle goes idle and off; while continuous goes begin_next (DOWN command gated by DownLimit). For continuous wrap, in same scan output MoveDown if !DownLimitSwitch. Hmm begin_next = \"post-phase DOWN with command gated by DownLimitSwitch\". So in the completion scan, phase becomes DOWN and MoveDown output asserted if not at down limit.\n\nIn continuous, when idle after emergency recovery? Continuous starts with Start edge from top-left. It runs forever.\n\nSingle step: Start edge triggers one phase, completion -> all off, busy false, NextAction advanced. Next Start works without top-left check except action 1's initial condition. Wait, how do we know a \"new automatic sequence\" vs subsequent step? When NextAction=1 (DOWN), require top-left pose. But after a full cycle in single-step, phase wraps from LEFT to DOWN; then next Start is new sequence requiring top-left pose. That matches.\n\nSo start condition: idle (busy false), selected automatic mode (SingleStep/SingleCycle/Continuous), rising Start, and if NextAction=1 then UpLimitSwitch & LeftLimitSwitch must both be true.\n\nThen busy true and execute current NextAction (which is 1 for DOWN).\n\nNow, \"Start edges during an active action are ignored; edge history still updates.\"\n\nMode change handling: selected mode is one of four, mutually exclusive. We store PrevMode (int 0..4 perhaps). On first invocation, modeChanged = FALSE. We need a FirstScan flag.\n\nMode change semantics: when selected mode differs from previous invocation, and not first invocation:\n- all outputs off this scan\n- sequence/latches cleared: NextAction=1, busy=false\n- edge memories updated (StartPrev := Start)\n- store PrevMode := selected mode.\n\nAlso what about mode change to no mode? \"No selected mode means all commands off and sequence reset.\" modeChanged definition: \"Selected mode differs from previous invocation\". If mode goes from manual to none, differs -> modeChanged true, off. Reset. Fine.\n\nWhat about EmergencyStop: \"EmergencyStop=FALSE cancels the sequence and outputs while retaining non-empty mode selection in arm_mode; changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence, and recovery requires a fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose (UpLimitSwitch & LeftLimitSwitch).\" Hmm this says emergency stop cancels sequence and outputs while retaining mode selection. Changing mode later clears outputs and resets sequence. Recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left.\n\nSo on emergency stop (safe false): outputs all off, sequence canceled => busy=false, NextAction=1? \"cancels the sequence and outputs\" yes reset sequence. Must require fresh Start edge from top-left. Need edge memory update while e-stop active to avoid stale edge. On release of e-stop, could a Start held high count as fresh edge? If Start was pressed during e-stop, edge history updates, so at recovery no rising edge -> safe. We should record StartPrev every scan including e-stop. Then recovery only when Start goes false->true after e-stop released, idle, top-left.\n\nDoes recovery require mode reselect? No, mode retained. Fresh start.\n\nPriority: emergency stop first; then mode change.\n\nNow \"No selected mode\" (none of four true) => off and sequence reset. modeChanged may be true when entering no mode; but also if no mode from first scan, modeChanged false but no_mode -> off and reset anyway. We can handle: if no mode, outputs off, reset busy, NextAction=1. Also mode memory still updates? selected mode 0. If no mode persists, modeChanged false. P1 uses no_mode -> off regardless.\n\nWhat about mode change between automatic modes while... suppressed anyway.\n\nManual mode with Start? Start irrelevant.\n\nNow think about outputs combination: \"Independent non-opposing actions may run together\" applies to manual only (combinational). Automatic only one command at a time. On a completion transition, output next command only, all others false.\n\nP4 reached limits: Every corresponding completion input suppresses its command. Important: even during modeChanged? off anyway. During manual, yes. During automatic, when limit reached we suppress command. But what about completion scan where next command is a different actuator whose limit is already reached? \"an already-active next limit suppresses its command but is processed as completion on the next invocation.\" So if next phase e.g. GRIP but GripLimitSwitch already true, ActivateGrip is suppressed; phase becomes GRIP; next invocation sees completion -> transitions to UP1. That means P4 holds globally: command outputs must be false whenever matching limit true. Our output logic should gate every command by its limit switch regardless.\n\nSo define:\nMoveDown := (some request for down) AND NOT DownLimitSwitch AND safe AND not modeChanged AND mode selected.\nMoveUp similarly, etc.\n\nRequests:\nManual mode:\n- down req = DownButton & !UpButton\n- up req = UpButton & !DownButton\n- left req = LeftButton & !RightButton\n- right req = RightButton & !LeftButton\n- grip req = GripButton & !ReleaseButton\n- release req = ReleaseButton & !GripButton\n\nAutomatic:\n- single step busy phase = DOWN: down req if DownLimit false\n- etc.\n- In completion scan for cycle modes, execute next phase's request.\n\nBut careful: at the completion scan for single-step, outputs are off, not next action. So no request.\n\nLet me formalize scan algorithm.\n\nState variables:\n- PrevMode : INT / enum (0 none,1 manual,2 step,3 single cycle,4 continuous)\n- FirstScan : BOOL := TRUE\n- NextAction : INT := 1 (1..8)\n- Busy : BOOL := FALSE\n- StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE (edge memory)\n- Maybe EStopPrev? Not required but could clear on e-stop.\n- Maybe ModeChanged output? Not interface, internal.\n\nOn each invocation:\n1. Determine selected mode int.\n2. Compute modeChanged := (NOT FirstScan) AND (SelectedMode <> PrevMode).\n3. Compute startRising := Start AND NOT StartPrev.\n\n4. Default outputs false, determine request flags.\n\n5. Priority:\nif NOT EmergencyStop (e-stop):\n   all outputs false\n   Busy := false\n   NextAction := 1\n   (maybe latch EStopLatched := TRUE?) The requirement: recovery requires fresh Start rising edge from top-left. If we reset NextAction=1 and Busy=false, a Start edge after e-stop release with top-left works. But if Start is held across release, StartPrev updated each scan (StartPrev := Start), so no rising edge. Good. Do we need latched state? Possibly after e-stop, the arm might not be at top-left. Start requires top-left. Fine.\n   \nelif no mode:\n   all off, Busy:=false, NextAction:=1.\n\nelif modeChanged:\n   all off, Busy:=false, NextAction:=1.\n   \nelse:\n   case selected mode:\n   Manual:\n      Busy:=false; NextAction:=1? \"Manual mode: allows individual actions\". Should sequence reset when entering manual? mode change resets sequence. While manual persists, sequence irrelevant. Could set NextAction:=1 each scan; yes safe.\n      Outputs based on buttons & limits & opposing.\n   SingleStep:\n      if Busy:\n         // phase active, check completion feedback\n         if completion(currentAction) then\n             // all outputs off (default)\n             Busy := false\n             NextAction := next(currentAction)\n         else\n             request current action command (gated by matching limit - but feedback false by else, so limit false for matching)\n      else:\n         // idle/waiting\n         if startRising AND (NextAction <> 1 OR (UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch)) then\n             Busy := true\n             // execute NextAction in this scan: request command\n             request phase NextAction\n         else\n             all off\n      end\n      \n      Hmm: start condition for action 1 only top-left. NextAction indicates action to execute next. At initial NextAction=1. After completing action 1, NextAction=2 and idle; next Start executes action 2 without pose check. After completing action 8, NextAction=1 and idle; next Start requires top-left (new sequence). Good.\n      \n   SingleCycle / Continuous:\n      if Busy:\n         if completion(currentAction):\n             if currentAction = LEFT (8):\n                if Continuous:\n                   NextAction := 1 (DOWN); Busy remains true; request DOWN command in same scan\n                else (SingleCycle):\n                   Busy := false; NextAction := 1; all outputs off\n             else:\n                NextAction := next; request next command in same scan; Busy true\n         else\n             request current command\n      else:\n         if startRising AND top-left:\n             Busy := true; NextAction:=1; request DOWN\n         else all off, NextAction stays (should be 1 if idle after cycle)\n      end\n      \nBut wait: at cycle completion for non-left phases, we \"switch to the next command in that scan\". So in scan where completion true (pre-phase X), output next command. Phase becomes next. If next limit already true, command suppressed. Next scan sees completion of next phase and advances again. That's consistent.\n\nAt LEFT completion in continuous wraps to DOWN in same scan, output MoveDown if !DownLimitSwitch. Busy true, NextAction=1.\n\nSingle cycle at LEFT completion: idle & off. NextAction=1.\n\nContinuous start at initial top-left: DOWN. Note if DownLimit already true at start? Top-left implies DownLimit false presumably (contradictory limits outside contract). Output gated.\n\nNow P6 \"start -> started (post-phase DOWN, busy TRUE)\". For single-step after action 1, Start triggers action 2, not down! Hmm P6 formula: G(safe & !modeChanged & start -> started) where \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". So `start` proposition includes top-left pose. Thus only a start from top-left (new sequence) counts, and post-phase DOWN. In single-step, subsequent starts without top-left aren't \"start\" atomic proposition. Good. Our behavior aligns: only from top-left do we assert DOWN. But careful: what if single-step waiting with NextAction=2 and also top-left pose true (e.g., arm is up-left from UP1 phase)? Then Start edge with top-left and NextAction=2: the atomic `start` says fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected auto mode -> started (post-phase DOWN busy). Hmm conflict! Let's think. In single-step sequence: after UP1 (action 3), arm is at top, and if horizontal at left? Actually action 4 RIGHT hasn't happened yet, so at top-left after UP1? Wait sequence: DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT. After UP1, position top-left (if left). So if operator presses Start for action 4, with top-left pose and NextAction=4, `start` proposition is true per definition: fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode. And P6 would require started = post-phase DOWN busy TRUE. But expected action 4 is RIGHT. Hmm contradiction. Unless `start` means a fresh start of a new sequence, i.e., NextAction=1 too. The definition says \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". In single-step, each step requires a fresh Start edge; but at action 4, after UP1, arm at top-left. That would make P6 force DOWN incorrectly.\n\nMaybe assumption \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE\" clarifies start of new sequence. The atomic `start` may be interpreted as new sequence start (idle + top-left + action1). In single-step, when waiting for next action after action 3, idle and top-left; `Start` edge is not a \"start\" of sequence because NextAction != 1. Hmm the proposition wording lacks NextAction. But P6 says \"A permitted fresh start begins down.\" Maybe \"start\" should include sequence reset/idle boundary state. The initial conditions include next_step=1; likely formal model's `start` predicate is defined as: single step idle waiting and Start rising, then post-phase should be DOWN? Wait but that can't be for subsequent steps.\n\nLet me reread: \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". Perhaps for single-step subsequent actions, after each action the arm often is not top-left except after UP1/UP2. But action 3 UP1 completion leaves at top and left (since RIGHT not yet). Then action 4 Start edge would indeed be top-left. Unless... LEFT position means left limit; yes it started left and hasn't moved right. So true.\n\nHmm, but maybe in single-step mode after each step, the step sequence is reset? No, \"The mechanical arm automatically executes one action according to the preset program, and the operator needs to confirm after each action is completed to execute the next action.\" Preset program progresses.\n\nCould there be an interpretation where after each single-step action, Start acts as \"execute next\" not \"start sequence\", and the formal `start` proposition is only about idle after a completed cycle (NextAction=1)? The explanation \"A permitted fresh start begins down\" and \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step)\" strongly suggests start predicate needs NextAction=1. I'll implement accordingly; formal verifier will tell.\n\nAlso note P7 `ordered`: \"the next accepted automatic phase follows DOWN, GRIP, UP1, RIGHT, DOWN2, RELEASE, UP2, LEFT\". Good.\n\nNow emergency stop interplay with outputs: on EStop false, off and reset. Also we should reset StartPrev memory each scan regardless.\n\nMode change detection: need to initialize PrevMode. First invocation: modeChanged false, PrevMode set to selected mode, then normal execution. If first invocation is manual buttons held, outputs should reflect buttons? \"The first invocation establishes the selected mode and executes normally.\" Yes.\n\nNo mode on first invocation: all off reset.\n\nLet me think about mode selection changes and Start edge. On modeChanged scan: clear sequence and suppress commands, edge memories update. So if Start rises exactly on mode-change scan, ignored, and StartPrev becomes true. On subsequent scans while Start still true, no edge. Operator must release and re-press. Good.\n\nOn e-stop scan: also update StartPrev. Sequence reset. Start edges ignored. After e-stop release with no mode change, if Start rising fresh from false->true and top-left -> start.\n\nNow what about Busy and NextAction reset on e-stop. NextAction:=1. Fine.\n\nNow need to define DUTs? Could implement with INT state and helper functions perhaps. Supporting objects allowed, but simplest single FB with VAR. Need to preserve exact interface. The declaration should be FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task and VAR sections. We can put internal variables in VAR. The instruction: \"declaration is CODESYS FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task followed by all VAR sections; omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK.\" So include input/output VAR sections and internal VAR.\n\nNeed supporting_objects list; can be empty. Probably easier empty unless we define enum. We can use INT constants. But maybe enum DUT nicer. To minimize compile risks, use INT values and local constants? CODESYS allows local CONSTANT VAR? e.g., VAR CONSTANT... END_VAR inside FB? Yes. But constants in declaration might be fine. Could simply use numeric comparisons. Let's define an enum DUT `E_ArmAction` maybe. Supporting object kind DUT with declaration. But compile may handle. Could also use INT. I'll use INT to be safe, with comments? Comments allowed in ST; user says no Markdown. Comments fine but not necessary. Better self-document.\n\nLet's design ST body carefully.\n\nVariables in declaration:\n\nVAR_INPUT\n    ManualMode : BOOL;\n    SingleStepMode : BOOL;\n    SingleCycleMode : BOOL;\n    ContinuousMode : BOOL;\n    EmergencyStop : BOOL;\n    Start : BOOL;\n    DownButton : BOOL;\n    UpButton : BOOL;\n    LeftButton : BOOL;\n    RightButton : BOOL;\n    GripButton : BOOL;\n    ReleaseButton : BOOL;\n    DownLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    UpLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    LeftLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    RightLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    GripLimitSwitch : BOOL;\n    ReleaseLimitSwitch : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nVAR_OUTPUT\n    MoveDown : BOOL;\n    MoveUp : BOOL;\n    MoveLeft : BOOL;\n    MoveRight : BOOL;\n    ActivateGrip : BOOL;\n    ActivateRelease : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nVAR\n    PrevMode : INT := 0;\n    FirstScan : BOOL := TRUE;\n    NextAction : INT := 1;\n    Busy : BOOL := FALSE;\n    StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE;\n    SelectedMode : INT;\n    ModeChanged : BOOL;\n    StartEdge : BOOL;\n    Done : BOOL;\n    ReqDown, ReqUp, ReqLeft, ReqRight, ReqGrip, ReqRelease : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n\nDo we need more? We compute outputs default false each scan.\n\nImplementation:\n\nSelectedMode := 0;\nIF ManualMode THEN SelectedMode := 1;\nELSIF SingleStepMode THEN SelectedMode := 2;\nELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN SelectedMode := 3;\nELSIF ContinuousMode THEN SelectedMode := 4;\nEND_IF\n\nModeChanged := (NOT FirstScan) AND (SelectedMode <> PrevMode);\nStartEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev;\n\nReqDown := FALSE; ... all false.\n\nIF NOT EmergencyStop THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSIF SelectedMode = 0 THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSIF ModeChanged THEN\n    Busy := FALSE;\n    NextAction := 1;\nELSE\n    CASE SelectedMode OF\n    1: // manual\n        Busy := FALSE;\n        NextAction := 1;\n        ReqDown := DownButton AND NOT UpButton;\n        ReqUp := UpButton AND NOT DownButton;\n        ReqLeft := LeftButton AND NOT RightButton;\n        ReqRight := RightButton AND NOT LeftButton;\n        ReqGrip := GripButton AND NOT ReleaseButton;\n        ReqRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT GripButton;\n    2: // single step\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := FALSE;\n            CASE NextAction OF\n                1: Done := DownLimitSwitch;\n                2: Done := GripLimitSwitch;\n                3: Done := UpLimitSwitch;\n                4: Done := RightLimitSwitch;\n                5: Done := DownLimitSwitch;\n                6: Done := ReleaseLimitSwitch;\n                7: Done := UpLimitSwitch;\n                8: Done := LeftLimitSwitch;\n            END_CASE\n            IF Done THEN\n                Busy := FALSE;\n                NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                IF NextAction > 8 THEN NextAction := 1; END_IF\n            ELSE\n                CASE NextAction OF\n                    1: ReqDown := TRUE;\n                    2: ReqGrip := TRUE;\n                    3: ReqUp := TRUE;\n                    4: ReqRight := TRUE;\n                    5: ReqDown := TRUE;\n                    6: ReqRelease := TRUE;\n                    7: ReqUp := TRUE;\n                    8: ReqLeft := TRUE;\n                END_CASE\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND ((NextAction <> 1) OR (UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch)) THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    3: // single cycle\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := ... CASE NextAction ...\n            IF Done THEN\n                IF NextAction = 8 THEN\n                    Busy := FALSE;\n                    NextAction := 1;\n                ELSE\n                    NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                    CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n                END_IF\n            ELSE\n                CASE NextAction OF ... request ...\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                NextAction := 1;\n                ReqDown := TRUE;\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    4: // continuous\n        IF Busy THEN\n            Done := ...\n            IF Done THEN\n                IF NextAction = 8 THEN\n                    NextAction := 1;\n                    Busy := TRUE;\n                    ReqDown := TRUE;\n                ELSE\n                    NextAction := NextAction + 1;\n                    request...\n                END_IF\n            ELSE\n                request...\n            END_IF\n        ELSE\n            IF StartEdge AND UpLimitSwitch AND LeftLimitSwitch THEN\n                Busy := TRUE;\n                NextAction := 1;\n                ReqDown := TRUE;\n            END_IF\n        END_IF\n    END_CASE\nEND_IF\n\nThen output gating:\n\nMoveDown := ReqDown AND EmergencyStop AND (SelectedMode <> 0) AND NOT ModeChanged AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;\nMoveUp := ReqUp AND ... AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;\n...\nActivateGrip := ReqGrip AND ... AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;\nActivateRelease := ReqRelease AND ... AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;\n\nNote manual opposing: ReqDown excludes UpButton; and ReqUp excludes DownButton. Good. Grip/release likewise.\n\n\"all_auto_steps_valid\" etc.\n\nThen state update:\nPrevMode := SelectedMode;\nFirstScan := FALSE;\nStartPrev := Start;\n\nImportant: On mode change, selected mode differs from previous. On no mode, selected 0.\n\nNow question: mode change from none to manual: modeChanged true, outputs suppressed first manual scan. Assumption says \"then a change of mode after the first invocation clears sequence/latches and suppresses commands for that invocation\". Yes, entering any mode suppresses outputs for that invocation. Even from no mode to manual. Fine.\n\nEmergency stop \"retaining non-empty mode selection in arm_mode; changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\". If e-stop active, Inputs mode selection may change; do we set PrevMode to SelectedMode each scan? Yes. If mode changes during e-stop, then modeChanged was true but e-stop branch had priority; still PrevMode updates, so no later modeChanged. Is that okay? \"changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\" - during e-stop outputs already off and sequence reset, so no difference. Fine.\n\nNow think about P10: G((safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> other_manual) & ((safe & modeChanged) -> off)).\nother_manual defines Up/Left/Right/Grip/Release exactly as manual formula. Our logic matches except no mode? ManualMode true means selected mode 1. good. Note manual request in our Code does not include limit in request but output gating includes NOT limit, matching formula. opposing buttons exactly.\n\nP3 manual down formula doesn't include EStop? `safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> (down <-> down_manual)` where down_manual lacks safe. Matches.\n\nP1: G((!safe | no_mode) -> off). Our e-stop and no mode output gating off. Even if modeChanged false etc. yes.\n\nP2 exclusive: Our automatic only requests one command; manual interlocks; gating doesn't create opposite. Fine.\n\nP4 reached limits: output gating with NOT corresponding limit. Fine.\n\nP5 step wait: step_done = SingleStepMode & pre-busy & matching feedback. In that scan, our Busy pre-state true and Done true -> outputs none (req all false). Good. waiting = SingleStepMode & !pre-busy & !rising(Start) -> off. Our idle -> no req. Good. Note if Start rising and cannot start (not top-left), outputs off. `waiting` excludes rising so P5 doesn't demand. Fine.\n\nP6 automatic start: start -> started. Our start from top-left in auto starts down. But single step with NextAction != 1 and top-left pose presses Start -> we execute that action, not started! As discussed potential issue. Need to decide whether formal tool's `start` predicate includes NextAction=1. The tool's atomic proposition definitions likely compiled manually by benchmark authors: \"start\": \"fresh Start edge in idle with top-left pose and selected automatic mode\". If literally implemented without NextAction, our action 4 start from top-left could violate P6. But their reference implementation must satisfy it, and likely they included NextAction=1 or \"new sequence\". Let's examine wording in assumption: \"At the start of a new automatic sequence (action 1, not every individual single step), UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch must both be TRUE.\" This implies NextAction=1 is known. P6 description \"A permitted fresh start begins down.\" A \"permitted fresh start\" is a new sequence. So likely `start` includes NextAction=1. I'll go with that.\n\nWait, but in single step after completing action 1 (DOWN), the arm is down; NextAction=2; Start executes GRIP. In idle, `start` prop per definition would require top-left pose, which is false, so P6 not triggered. After action 3 (UP1), top-left true and NextAction=4; press Start executes RIGHT. Physically at top-left but perhaps \"start\" should be false because NextAction != 1. If not, violation. Again likely includes sequence idle phase 1.\n\nCould avoid ambiguity by resetting NextAction? No, we must execute RIGHT after UP1 in single-step. So can't satisfy literal P6 otherwise. Trust assumption.\n\nNow think about \"Only one phase transition occurs per invocation.\" Our code one transition max. In continuous LEFT->DOWN same scan. Fine.\n\nNow about the state after single-step completion: NextAction advances. We set outputs off. Next Start executes next. Good.\n\nNow \"initial position top left\" doesn't force initial outputs; outputs zero initially. The FB var default outputs false. Good P_init.\n\nNow could formal verifier require no mode => sequence reset but not mode change? We do reset each no-mode scan. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: StartPrev update on very first scan. Start high on first scan: StartEdge false since StartPrev initialized FALSE; so no start. Is that expected? \"A Start press is a rising edge.\" First invocation with Start already TRUE — is that an edge? Probably no, because edge memory starts FALSE, so StartPrev FALSE -> StartEdge TRUE! Wait StartEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev, with StartPrev init FALSE. On first scan Start=TRUE gives StartEdge TRUE. Is that a rising edge? The memory starts FALSE, so yes technically. But \"fresh Start edge\" likely yes. The first invocation starts normally. \"The first invocation establishes the selected mode and executes normally.\" So if Start held on first scan and top-left, it may start. Hmm, is that acceptable? Test frameworks often reset instance and set Start false initially. First invocation with Start TRUE may be considered rising from initial false. Fine. In our initialization StartPrev FALSE -> edge. Good.\n\nNow think about modes exclusive assumption. No need to validate >1; caller keeps mutually exclusive. Our ELSIF priority would choose manual if multiple, but verifier won't test multiple per assumption (rejected). Fine.\n\nNow compile and simulation. Need use compile_st max 3 calls, simulate max 3, verify. Save final candidate.json. We have read-only FS but \"save exact final submitted source to candidate.json\" — likely we need write file; sandbox is read-only though tools perhaps? shell commands unnecessary. Hmm. We may need to use apply_patch? No write permitted? The environment says read-only, but the task says save candidate.json. Perhaps we can use shell? It says shell commands unnecessary but we might need to save. We can use apply_patch? apply_patch not listed as tool in this environment, only shell_command. Actually tools available: shell_command, plan, MCP compile/sim/verify, etc. No apply_patch tool despite developer mention. Sandbox read-only and approval never. We can't write via shell? Perhaps shell commands can write? Permission profile restricted read only, so no. But maybe the candidate.json is generated by harness? Hmm. We should attempt to write at end with shell_command? It may fail. Could use compile tool? No. Maybe there is an existing file? Let's inspect? The task says shell commands unnecessary. But requirement \"Save the exact final submitted source to candidate.json\" likely evaluated from our answer? We'll try write with PowerShell at the end; if denied, still return JSON. We can see.\n\nFirst let's maybe list files to see if candidate.json exists? \"Do not inspect other directories\" but current dir perhaps contains package. We shouldn't need. Let's start with compile_st. Need provide source object declaration, implementation, supporting_objects. Let's write clean ST.\n\nOne important CODESYS syntax nuance: Multiple variables on one VAR line `ReqDown, ReqUp, ... : BOOL;` is allowed in CODESYS? Yes, comma-separated identifiers allowed. But to be safe, one per line.\n\nCASE with integer. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `NOT EmergencyStop` etc.\n\nLet's consider output gating and modeChanged: If modeChanged true, req all false anyway; but gate too.\n\nWe might not need Done variable; but okay.\n\nLet's think about emergency stop definition: \"FALSE: Emergency stop triggered; TRUE: No emergency stop triggered.\" safe = EmergencyStop. Good.\n\nNow what about Start edge during active action: \"ignored; edge history still updates.\" We compute StartEdge every scan but only use in idle. StartPrev := Start at end. Good.\n\n\"Only one phase transition per invocation\" - In single step idle start, we set Busy true and request phase. That's a transition from idle to active. In continuous completion, one phase transition plus wrap. Fine.\n\nNow \"In cycle modes, completion feedback switches to the next command in that scan; an already-active next limit suppresses its command but is processed as completion on the next invocation.\" Our code: Done true -> NextAction++ and request next unconditionally (gated by limit in output). If next limit true, output gated off. Next scan Busy true, Done uses that limit -> transitions. Correct.\n\nBut what about at completion of phase 8 in continuous and DownLimit already true? Output MoveDown gated off; next scan Done down -> transition to GRIP. Correct.\n\nAt start (idle->DOWN) if DownLimit true (top-left shouldn't, but if contradictory) output gated off; next scan busy DOWN and Done -> GRIP. Fine.\n\nNow single cycle start requirement top-left: We require UpLimit & LeftLimit. Good. Note initial position top-left.\n\nSingle-step action 1 requirement top-left. Subsequent steps no check. Good.\n\nNow \"manual mode provided it is not yet in position\" — our down etc. checks matching limit. For grip, \"not yet in position\" GripLimitSwitch. Good.\n\n\"Independent non-opposing actions may run together\" — e.g., down + grip simultaneously; our manual requests independent. Opposing on same axis inhibited, grip/release inhibited. Vertical and horizontal allowed simultaneously. But wait limit-related P4 suppresses command if limit. Fine.\n\nNow potential interference: In manual, if DownButton and UpButton both pressed, both req false. P3 down_manual includes `DownButton AND NOT UpButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch`; our MoveDown = reqDown AND ... NOT DownLimit; reqDown = DownButton AND NOT UpButton, so both false. good.\n\nNow formal condition P10 other_manual for Up/Left/Right/Grip/Release. Our output gating also includes safe etc. Formula only when safe & not modeChanged. good.\n\nWhat about output gating `SelectedMode <> 0` redundant when ManualMode.\n\nNow let's think of scenario \"changing mode later clears outputs and resets the sequence\" during e-stop. Our e-stop resets. Fine.\n\nOne subtlety: After e-stop, \"recovery requires a fresh Start rising edge from top-left pose\". But our Busy=false NextAction=1. After release, if Start edge high and top-left, starts. If mode is manual, Start irrelevant. If mode is no mode, off. Good.\n\nNow modeChanged should be FALSE for first invocation. We use FirstScan. On first scan if SelectedMode=0, modeChanged false; no mode off. Fine.\n\nDo we need to retain selected mode in arm_mode even if no mode? \"No selected mode means all commands off and sequence reset.\" fine.\n\nNow write implementation. Let's be meticulous about variable declarations and semicolons.\n\nOne nuance: In CODESYS, assigning outputs within CASE? We assign Req flags and compute outputs after. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: use of `ELSIF` supported. Yes.\n\nPotential issue: `CASE SelectedMode OF 1: ... 2: ... END_CASE` with statements. Good.\n\nLet's create helper procedure? Inline repetition. Could use helper methods but not necessary.\n\nNow formal verify might detect something. Let's think about P9 liveness. Formula: (G(safe & SingleCycleMode) & G(busy -> F feedback)) -> G(started -> F idle). Our single cycle runs through phases; if feedback eventually each busy, returns idle. Yes, assuming mode unchanged. If e-stop? Premise G(safe & SingleCycleMode) holds, so no. If mode changed? Then G(safe & SingleCycleMode) would be false after change, premise false. So fine. Need idle eventually after started. We progress. Good.\n\nBut subtle: P9 says feedback eventually for each busy; we do transition. okay.\n\nP8 cycle boundary: G((safe & !modeChanged) -> ((cycle_done -> (idle & off)) & (continuous_done -> begin_next))). cycle_done = SingleCycleMode AND pre-phase LEFT AND LeftLimitSwitch. Our if Done and NextAction=8 -> Busy=false, outputs off (req all false). idle post = post-sequence idle. yes. continuous_done -> begin_next = post-phase DOWN with command gated by DownLimit. Our NextAction=1 Busy true ReqDown true, gated. yes.\n\nP7 ordered. We follow.\n\nP6 start -> started. Need start predicate as interpreted. Fine.\n\nP5 step wait. In single step pre-busy and matching feedback -> off. Our Done branch outputs off. waiting -> off. Good.\n\nBut in single-step waiting, if Start rising but fails top-left for action 1, outputs off; fine since \"waiting\" excludes rising, P5 doesn't constrain. Maybe there are other specs? no.\n\nNow P3 manual down exact equivalence: G(safe & !modeChanged & ManualMode -> (down <-> down_manual)).\ndown = MoveDown output. down_manual = ManualMode AND DownButton AND NOT UpButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch.\nOur MoveDown when ManualMode & !modeChanged & safe = (DownButton AND NOT UpButton) AND NOT DownLimitSwitch. Exact. yes.\n\nP4 reached limits exact: our gating ensures any MoveDown true only if !DownLimitSwitch. yes.\n\nP2 exclusive: situations:\n- Manual down req and left req can be simultaneous (down+left) allowed; exclusive only opposes down/up and left/right and grip/release. Our per-axis opposing prevents both. good.\n- Automatic one req.\n- Could automatic req down and something else? Only one CASE branch. no.\n- In manual, could ReqDown and ReqUp both? ReqDown includes !UpButton, ReqUp includes !DownButton. no.\n- Grip/release same.\n\nNow one concern: outputs latching? We assign all outputs every scan after computing flags, so no stale. Good.\n\nNow declaration first line exactly `FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task`. Then VAR_INPUT etc. Omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. The JSON field declaration should include sections but no final END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. Fine.\n\nsupporting_objects [].\n\nLet's compile via mcp__plc__compile_st."}],"status":"completed"},"sequence_number":2922}

